const e=[{id:1,code:"RED-01",icon:"Flame",accent:"#dc2626",category:"Emergency",title:"Code Red — Fire Response",subtitle:"Protecting the lives of patients and staff during a hospital fire",summary:"Code Red is the code a hospital uses to announce a fire. A fast, organized response protects patients' lives, because many of them cannot get out on their own. Staff work by the RACE procedure: Rescue, Alarm, Contain, and Extinguish or Evacuate. Fire extinguishers are used with the PASS technique. This lesson explains how a fire is recognized, how the alarm is raised, and how a fire is safely contained and put out.",facts:[{label:"Emergency numbers",value:"Hospital internal emergency number + 115"},{label:"Standard",value:"NFPA 101 + NFPA 99"},{label:"Response procedure",value:"RACE + PASS"},{label:"Extinguisher for electrical equipment",value:"CO₂ or clean agent"}],steps:[{title:"R — Rescue",detail:"Immediately move patients near the fire and smoke out of danger. Move a bedbound patient in their bed after releasing the wheel locks. Move patients on oxygen or a ventilator with a portable oxygen cylinder or a bag-mask device.",critical:!0},{title:"A — Alarm",detail:'Pull or push the fire alarm pull station and call the hospital internal emergency number. In a calm voice say "Code Red" and give the location of the fire (floor and room). Civil Defense (115) is notified according to the hospital fire plan.',critical:!0},{title:"C — Contain",detail:"Close the doors of the fire room and the corridor so smoke and fire do not spread, but do not lock them. Do not shut the zone oxygen valve immediately: it is closed only after patients on oxygen and those on ventilators have been switched to portable cylinders or bag-mask, and only on the order of the responsible physician or nurse or according to the fire plan.",critical:!0},{title:"E — Extinguish",detail:"Only if all of this is true: the fire is small and just starting (no larger than a wastebasket), the extinguisher is the right type, your exit route behind you is clear, the alarm has been raised, you have been trained and no smoke or heat blocks your way out. If the fire does not go out at once, leave and close the door behind you. Choose the right extinguisher: CO₂ for electrical equipment, wet chemical (Class K) for cooking oil, and in the MRI room only a non-magnetic extinguisher."},{title:"E — Evacuate",detail:`If the fire is large or cannot be controlled, move patients horizontally to a safe compartment behind smoke doors on the same floor. If necessary, then move them vertically to a lower floor.
• Do not use elevators.`,critical:!0}],reference:[{heading:"The right extinguisher for each location",points:["Operating room (OR): saline for a fire on the patient, and a CO₂ extinguisher (ASA recommendation)","MRI: only a non-magnetic extinguisher labeled for MRI; a steel extinguisher becomes a projectile","Laboratory: CO₂ or ABC depending on the materials present; a Class D unit whose label lists the metal, where combustible metals are present","ICU: CO₂ or clean agent for electrical equipment","Kitchen and cafeteria: wet chemical (Class K)","Corridors and public areas: ABC powder"]},{heading:"PASS — Extinguisher technique",points:["Before PASS, every answer must be yes: the fire is small and just starting, the extinguisher is the right type, your exit is behind you, the alarm has been raised, you are trained and no smoke or heat blocks your way out","P = Pull: pull the safety pin","A = Aim: aim the nozzle at the base of the fire, not at the top of the flames","S = Squeeze: squeeze the handle firmly","S = Sweep: sweep the nozzle from side to side; if the fire does not go out at once, leave and close the door","Small extinguishers empty quickly, often in a few seconds (the exact time is on the label). Start from the distance on the label, with your exit behind you, and move closer only as the fire gets smaller"]},{heading:"What must not be done",points:["Do not use the elevator: it may stop at the fire floor","Do not open windows: extra air makes the fire burn more intensely","Do not take a steel extinguisher into the MRI room: the magnet pulls it with great force","Do not pour water on energized electrical equipment: risk of a fatal shock","Do not pour water on hot oil: the oil spatters violently and the fire spreads"]}],warnings:["The first step is always to rescue the people in danger. Do not shut the zone oxygen valve immediately: first switch patients on oxygen or ventilators to portable cylinders or bag-mask, then close the valve on the order of the clinical lead or according to the fire plan. Use an extinguisher only on a small fire; if the fire is larger than a wastebasket, evacuate the area."],realCase:"Practical case (training example): In a hospital laboratory, a bottle of 70% alcohol caught fire on the workbench. A trained nurse immediately applied RACE: 1) moved nearby people away, 2) announced Code Red on the internal number, 3) with her exit kept open behind her, put out the small fire with a CO₂ extinguisher using PASS before it could spread, 4) left, closed the laboratory door and had the room ventilated before anyone went back in. Because CO₂ leaves no residue, the laboratory equipment was not damaged. Had ABC powder been used, cleaning the equipment would have been much more difficult.",caseStudy:[{prompt:"You work in the ICU. Suddenly smoke comes out of an IV pump and a small flame appears on its cable. A patient on a ventilator is 2 meters away. What do you do first?",options:[{text:"I pick up the CO₂ extinguisher and start putting out the fire using PASS",correct:!1,feedback:"Too early. The patient is in danger; the first step is Rescue (R)."},{text:"I open the room door to let the air change",correct:!1,feedback:"Dangerous: extra air makes the fire burn more intensely and spreads the smoke."},{text:"With my colleague, I connect the patient to a bag-mask or portable cylinder and move their bed away from the fire",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: Rescue (R). Once the patient is protected, the alarm comes next."},{text:"I call the elevator to move the patient",correct:!1,feedback:"Elevators must not be used during a Code Red; they may stop."}]},{prompt:"The patient has been moved out of danger. The fire is still small and only a few seconds have passed since it started. What is the second step?",options:[{text:"I pick up the extinguisher and start putting out the fire",correct:!1,feedback:"In RACE, the alarm comes before extinguishing, so that the backup teams know."},{text:"I push the fire alarm pull station and call the internal emergency number",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: Alarm (A). Code Red must be announced before extinguishing."},{text:"I call a friend in the unit on my personal phone",correct:!1,feedback:"This is not the official route. The fire alarm and the internal number alert the Code Red team, and the hospital notifies 115 according to its plan."},{text:"I draw the curtain at the door",correct:!1,feedback:"This is part of Contain, not Alarm. The alarm comes before containment."}]},{prompt:"The fire is still small (smaller than a wastebasket), and a CO₂ extinguisher is within reach 3 meters away. What is the next step?",options:[{text:"With my exit kept open behind me, I put out the small fire with the CO₂ extinguisher; if it does not go out at once, I leave and close the door behind me",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: extinguish only while the fire is small and your exit is open; if it does not go out, leave and close the door (Contain). CO₂ is suitable for electrical equipment fires and leaves no residue; in a small room, ventilate after use."},{text:"I pick up the ABC extinguisher because it works on all fires",correct:!1,feedback:"ABC works on electrical fires, but its powder contaminates medical equipment and irritates patients' airways. In the ICU a CO₂ extinguisher is better."},{text:"I pour water on it",correct:!1,feedback:"Dangerous: the cable is still energized and an electric shock will occur."},{text:"I open the door to let the air change",correct:!1,feedback:"Opening the door to change the air feeds the fire with oxygen and spreads smoke. Keep only your way out clear, and close the door behind you when you leave."}]}],quiz:[{question:"In the ICU, what is the first step during a Code Red?",options:[{text:"I pick up the extinguisher",correct:!1,feedback:"Before extinguishing, the patient must be out of danger."},{text:"I rescue the patients in danger, and connect those on oxygen to a portable cylinder or bag-mask",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: RACE begins with Rescue. The zone oxygen valve is closed later, on the order of the person in charge."},{text:"I open the windows",correct:!1,feedback:"This increases the air supply and makes the fire bigger."},{text:"I wait for the 115 team",correct:!1,feedback:"Do not wait for the team; start RACE immediately."}]},{question:"Which extinguisher is suitable in the MRI room?",options:[{text:"A standard (steel) ABC extinguisher",correct:!1,feedback:"A steel extinguisher is pulled strongly by the magnet and becomes a dangerous projectile."},{text:"A non-magnetic extinguisher labeled for MRI (MR Conditional)",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: only non-magnetic equipment may enter the MRI room (Zone IV). The MRI magnet stays active even during a power outage."},{text:"Water in a steel bucket",correct:!1,feedback:"A steel bucket also becomes a projectile, and water on electrical equipment is dangerous."},{text:"A standard CO₂ extinguisher from the corridor",correct:!1,feedback:"Standard corridor extinguishers are generally steel and must not be taken into the MRI room."}]},{question:"What does the letter C stand for in RACE?",options:[{text:"Call",correct:!1,feedback:"Calling is part of A (Alarm)."},{text:"Contain (confining the fire)",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: closing doors so smoke and fire do not spread."},{text:"Cool",correct:!1,feedback:"Incorrect; C stands for Contain."},{text:"Carry",correct:!1,feedback:"Incorrect; C stands for Contain."}]},{question:"About how long does a small (2 kg) extinguisher last?",options:[{text:"Only a few seconds; the exact time is on its label",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: a very short time, so you must aim accurately at the base of the fire. The exact discharge time is on the label."},{text:"5 minutes",correct:!1,feedback:"Far too long; a small extinguisher empties within a few seconds."},{text:"2–3 minutes",correct:!1,feedback:"Still too long; a small extinguisher empties within a few seconds (see the label)."},{text:"One hour",correct:!1,feedback:"Incorrect."}]}]},{id:2,code:"BLUE-02",icon:"HeartPulse",accent:"#2563eb",category:"Emergency",title:"Code Blue — Cardiac Resuscitation",subtitle:"Advanced CPR, the AED and the work of the Code Blue team",summary:"Code Blue is the code announced in a hospital when a patient's heart or breathing stops. Every minute of delay in cardiopulmonary resuscitation (CPR) and defibrillation lowers the chance of survival. Under AHA 2025, the check for breathing and a pulse takes no more than 10 seconds, and CPR then starts immediately. This lesson explains advanced CPR, use of the automated external defibrillator (AED), and the roles of the Code Blue team.",facts:[{label:"Chest compressions",value:"100–120 per minute"},{label:"Compression depth",value:"5–6 cm (adult)"},{label:"CPR ratio",value:"30:2 (compressions:breaths)"},{label:"Standard",value:"AHA 2025"}],steps:[{title:"1. Recognize the arrest (in less than 10 seconds)",detail:"The patient is unresponsive, is not breathing or is only gasping, and has no carotid pulse. The check must not take longer than 10 seconds.",critical:!0},{title:"2. Call a Code Blue",detail:`Press the Code Blue button on the wall or call the hospital internal emergency number.
• Do not wait for the team: start CPR immediately.`,critical:!0},{title:"3. CPR at 30:2",detail:"Place the heels of your hands on the lower half of the breastbone, keep your arms straight, and compress to a depth of 5–6 cm at a rate of 100–120 per minute. Allow the chest to recoil fully. After every 30 compressions, give 2 breaths with a bag-mask device.",critical:!0},{title:"4. Use the AED",detail:`Turn the device on and place the two pads on the bare chest as shown on the pads: one on the upper right side of the chest and one on the lower left side. The device analyzes the heart rhythm by itself.
• No one touches the patient during analysis or the shock.`,critical:!0},{title:"5. Code Blue team roles",detail:"Physician: team leader. Nurse 1: chest compressions. Nurse 2: vascular access (IV/IO) and medications. Technician: AED or defibrillator. Recorder: documents times, medications and shocks. The person doing compressions is replaced every 2 minutes."},{title:"6. Medications",detail:"Epinephrine 1 mg IV/IO every 3–5 minutes. Amiodarone 300 mg for VF or pVT that persists after shocks (second dose 150 mg). Atropine is for bradycardia, not for cardiac arrest."}],reference:[{heading:"CPR for adults, children and infants",points:["Adult (from puberty onward): two hands, 5–6 cm, 30:2","Child (from 1 year to puberty): one or two hands, about 5 cm (one third of the chest depth), 30:2 with one rescuer and 15:2 with two rescuers","Infant (under 1 year): two thumbs encircling the chest or the heel of one hand, about 4 cm, 30:2 with one rescuer and 15:2 with two rescuers","Switch the person doing compressions every 2 minutes so the compressions stay strong"]},{heading:"AED — four steps",points:["1. Turn the device on and follow its voice prompts","2. Place the two pads on the bare chest as shown on the pads","3. No one touches the patient during analysis or the shock","4. Resume CPR immediately after the shock; the device will announce when it re-analyzes the rhythm"]},{heading:"Special situations",points:["Pregnant woman: manually displace the uterus to the left to reduce pressure on the great vessels","Electric shock: cut off the power before touching the patient","Anaphylaxis: epinephrine IM into the outer thigh","Severe bleeding: direct pressure on the wound, and vascular access for fluids and blood","Patient on a ventilator: ventilate with a bag-mask during transport"]}],warnings:["Do not wait for the team: start CPR immediately. Every minute of delay in CPR and defibrillation lowers the chance of survival. Press the Code Blue button and start CPR at the same time, or send someone else to raise the alarm."],realCase:"Practical case (training example): A 62-year-old patient in the ICU after cardiac surgery went into cardiac arrest at 22:14. The shift nurse pressed the Code Blue button at 22:14:07 and immediately started CPR at 30:2. The Code Blue team arrived at 22:14:45. At 22:15:12 the AED detected VF and delivered the first shock. After two shocks and about 4 minutes of CPR, the heart started beating again at 22:19 (ROSC). The patient stayed in the ICU for post-cardiac arrest care and left the ICU in good condition a few days later. This example shows how important it is to start CPR within the first seconds and to defibrillate early.",caseStudy:[{prompt:"You are the ICU shift nurse. At 23:40 a patient's monitor shows V-tach, which then becomes a flat line. You go to the bedside: the patient is unresponsive and not breathing. What do you do first?",options:[{text:"I call the on-duty physician to make a diagnosis",correct:!1,feedback:"Delaying CPR for a diagnosis lowers the chance of survival. The situation is clear: cardiac arrest."},{text:"I press the Code Blue button and immediately start CPR at 30:2",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: Code Blue and CPR at the same time. Do not wait for the team."},{text:"I look for the AED before starting CPR",correct:!1,feedback:"Start CPR immediately; a colleague can bring the AED."},{text:"I prepare epinephrine",correct:!1,feedback:"Medications are the Code Blue team's task after CPR has started. Chest compressions come first."}]},{prompt:'CPR has been going on for about 2 minutes. The Code Blue team has arrived and the AED pads are on. The device says: "Shock advised." What do you do?',options:[{text:"I continue chest compressions during the shock as well",correct:!1,feedback:"Dangerous: the current will strike you too. No one touches the patient during the shock."},{text:'I call out loudly "Clear!", make sure no one is touching the patient, and then deliver the shock',correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: everyone stands clear. CPR resumes immediately after the shock."},{text:"After the shock, I wait for the pulse to return (ROSC)",correct:!1,feedback:"Immediately after the shock, 2 minutes of CPR are given; do not wait."},{text:"I remove the AED and focus on IV access",correct:!1,feedback:"The AED must stay attached to the patient. The rhythm is re-analyzed after 2 minutes of CPR."}]},{prompt:"After two shocks and 4 minutes of CPR, the patient shows ROSC: the pulse and breathing return. What is the next step?",options:[{text:"I stop CPR; our work is done",correct:!1,feedback:"After ROSC, close monitoring is needed: monitor, airway, breathing and blood pressure."},{text:"Continuous monitoring, assessment of airway, breathing and circulation (ABC), and post-cardiac arrest care in the ICU",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: post-cardiac arrest care also includes temperature control."},{text:"I discharge the patient from the hospital",correct:!1,feedback:"The patient needs intensive care in the ICU."},{text:"I give another 1 mg dose of epinephrine to sustain ROSC",correct:!1,feedback:"After ROSC, the cardiac arrest dose (1 mg) is not given. If blood pressure is low, the physician may order a vasopressor infusion."}]}],quiz:[{question:"What is the correct rate of chest compressions?",options:[{text:"60–80 per minute",correct:!1,feedback:"Too slow; not enough blood reaches the brain."},{text:"100–120 per minute",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: according to AHA 2025."},{text:"More than 150 per minute",correct:!1,feedback:"Too fast; the heart does not fill completely and compression depth decreases."},{text:"Any rate",correct:!1,feedback:"The rate matters for effective CPR."}]},{question:"How is CPR performed on an infant (under 1 year)?",options:[{text:"Two hands, 6 cm",correct:!1,feedback:"Far too deep for an infant."},{text:"Two thumbs encircling the chest (or the heel of one hand), about 4 cm",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: under AHA 2025, the two-finger technique is no longer taught."},{text:"One hand, 5 cm",correct:!1,feedback:"This is for children from 1 year to puberty."},{text:"CPR is not performed on infants",correct:!1,feedback:"Incorrect; every infant in cardiac arrest receives CPR."}]},{question:"When does the AED not deliver a shock?",options:[{text:'When the device says "No shock advised"',correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: the AED delivers a shock only for VF and pulseless VT."},{text:"A shock is always given at the start",correct:!1,feedback:"Incorrect: asystole is not treated with a shock."},{text:"Always before CPR",correct:!1,feedback:"CPR starts immediately, and the AED is used as soon as it arrives."},{text:"Only for adults",correct:!1,feedback:"The AED is also used for children (with pediatric pads if available)."}]},{question:"What is the first drug given in a Code Blue?",options:[{text:"Atropine 1 mg",correct:!1,feedback:"Atropine is for bradycardia, not for cardiac arrest."},{text:"Epinephrine 1 mg IV/IO every 3–5 minutes",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: according to AHA."},{text:"Amiodarone 300 mg",correct:!1,feedback:"It is for VF or pVT that persists after shocks, not the first drug in every case."},{text:"Bicarbonate",correct:!1,feedback:"Only in special situations, such as hyperkalemia."}]}]},{id:3,code:"ORANGE-03",icon:"FireExtinguisher",accent:"#ea580c",category:"Fire",title:"Fire Classes and Extinguishers in the Hospital — NFPA 10",subtitle:"Fire classes under NFPA 10 and choosing the right extinguisher for medical areas",summary:"Fires in hospitals have their own characteristics: oxygen, alcohol, electrical medical equipment and rooms such as MRI. Fire classes and extinguisher selection are defined by NFPA 10 (the standard for portable fire extinguishers), and NFPA 99 (the Health Care Facilities Code) sets specific procedures for oxygen and the operating room. Choosing the wrong extinguisher can injure a patient or put critical equipment out of service. In the operating room, fire spreads very quickly in an oxygen-enriched atmosphere, so a correct and rapid response saves lives. Classes here use NFPA 10 letters as the project reference; the official system for Duhok is not yet confirmed. EN 2 equivalents: NFPA A = EN A; NFPA B (liquids) = EN B; NFPA B (gases) = EN C; NFPA C (live electrical equipment) has no EN class; NFPA D = EN D; NFPA K = EN F.",facts:[{label:"Standard",value:"NFPA 10 (extinguishers) + NFPA 99"},{label:"Fire classes (NFPA)",value:"A · B · C · D · K"},{label:"OEA (oxygen-enriched atmosphere)",value:"O₂ above 23.5%"},{label:"Temperature of an electrical arc",value:"Up to about 19,400°C"}],steps:[{title:"Class A — Ordinary solid materials",detail:`Paper, bed linen, fabric and plastic. Very common in hospitals. Extinguisher: water or ABC powder.
• Near oxygen, these materials burn much faster.`,critical:!0},{title:"Class B — Flammable liquids and gases",detail:`Alcohol (such as hand sanitizer gel), acetone and ether. The flash point of pure ethanol is about 13°C, and that of ether is about −45°C. Extinguisher for alcohol, acetone and other polar solvents: CO₂, ABC or alcohol-resistant (AR) foam; ordinary foam breaks down on them. Gas: stop the gas first if it is safe; never put out a gas flame you cannot stop.
• Do not pour water on flammable liquids.`,critical:!0},{title:"Class C — Energized electrical equipment",detail:`MRI, CT, X-ray, ventilators, defibrillators. If you can do so safely, cut off the power. Extinguisher: CO₂ or clean agent; ABC powder also works but contaminates the equipment.
• In the MRI room, only a non-magnetic extinguisher.`,critical:!0},{title:"Class D and lithium batteries",detail:`Class D covers combustible metals (such as magnesium and sodium in laboratories) and requires a Class D extinguisher or agent whose label lists that metal; the agent differs from metal to metal. The lithium-ion (Li-ion) batteries in medical devices, power wheelchairs and phones contain no metallic lithium: water and cooling are recommended for them. Class D is only for lithium-metal batteries.
• A Li-ion battery can reignite after being extinguished (thermal runaway); keep cooling it and monitor it.`,critical:!0},{title:"Class K — Cooking oil",detail:`Oils and fats in the kitchen and cafeteria. Wet chemical extinguisher (NFPA Class K; Class F on EN 2 labels) only.
• Water on hot oil makes burning oil spatter.
• An ABC extinguisher is not suitable for a cooking oil fire, because the oil reignites.`},{title:"OEA — Oxygen-enriched atmosphere",detail:"Under NFPA 99, air with an oxygen concentration above 23.5% is an OEA. In an OEA, materials ignite easily and fire spreads very quickly. In the operating room and ICU, keep cylinders away from heat sources. If a fire breaks out on a patient, the anesthesiologist immediately stops the oxygen flow to the patient.",critical:!0}],reference:[{heading:"Selection table (NFPA 10)",points:["A: water or ABC","B: liquids: CO₂, ABC or foam (alcohol-resistant foam for alcohol and acetone); gas: stop the gas first if it is safe","C: CO₂ or clean agent; ABC is also acceptable but contaminates equipment","D: only a Class D extinguisher or agent listed for that metal","K: wet chemical only"]},{heading:"Extinguishers by hospital department",points:["Operating room: CO₂ (ASA recommendation), and saline for a fire on the patient","ICU: CO₂ or clean agent for electrical equipment","MRI: only a non-magnetic extinguisher labeled for MRI","Laboratory: CO₂ or ABC, a Class D unit listed for the metal where combustible metals are present, and an eyewash station nearby","Pharmacy and storerooms: ABC, with flammable liquids kept in dedicated cabinets"]},{heading:"Operating room fire — first steps",points:["1. The anesthesiologist stops the flow of all airway gases (O₂ and N₂O)","2. Remove the drapes and all burning material from the patient","3. Put out the fire on the patient with saline or by smothering it","4. If the fire persists: a CO₂ extinguisher","5. If it is not extinguished: Code Red, evacuate the patient, close the door and shut the room's medical gas valve"]}],warnings:["In the operating room, fire spreads within seconds in an oxygen-enriched atmosphere. First stop the oxygen flow to the patient at the anesthesia machine and remove burning materials, then put out the fire. Do not take a steel extinguisher into the MRI room: the magnet pulls it in and it becomes a projectile."],realCase:"Practical case (training example): In an operating room, the alcohol-based skin prep had not yet dried when the electrocautery was used, and the drapes caught fire. A staff member brought an ABC extinguisher, and the powder spread over the patient's open wound and airway. The correct procedure would have been: stop the oxygen, remove the burning drapes, extinguish with saline and, if needed, use a CO₂ extinguisher. Lesson: before electrocautery is used, the prep solution must be completely dry.",caseStudy:[{prompt:"You are an operating room nurse. During surgery, the electrocautery ignites the alcohol-based prep solution and flames appear on the patient's chest. The patient is receiving 100% oxygen. What is the first step?",options:[{text:"I spray the ABC extinguisher on the patient",correct:!1,feedback:"Dangerous: the powder gets into the wound and the airway. ASA recommends a CO₂ extinguisher, and only after the gas flow has been stopped."},{text:"I pour water on it",correct:!1,feedback:"Saline can put out a fire on the patient, but until the oxygen is stopped the fire will reignite. The first step is stopping the oxygen."},{text:"I ask the anesthesiologist to stop the oxygen flow to the patient immediately",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: without excess oxygen the fire becomes much weaker. This is done at the anesthesia machine, not by shutting the zone valve."},{text:"I start horizontal evacuation",correct:!1,feedback:"The patient is on the operating table; first stop the oxygen and put out the fire."}]},{prompt:"The oxygen has been stopped, but the fire is still on the chest. What is the second step?",options:[{text:"I pick up the ABC extinguisher",correct:!1,feedback:"ABC powder contaminates the wound and the airway. If an extinguisher is needed, use CO₂."},{text:"I remove the burning drapes and put out the fire with saline or by smothering it",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: following ASA guidance, remove the burning materials and extinguish with saline."},{text:"I take the patient down off the table",correct:!1,feedback:"Dangerous: the patient will be injured and the fire will spread."},{text:"I wait for the Code Red team",correct:!1,feedback:"Far too late; saline and wet cloths are within your reach."}]},{prompt:"The fire is out. What is the third step?",options:[{text:"I continue the surgery as before",correct:!1,feedback:"Incorrect: the patient must be fully assessed and the incident must be documented."},{text:"I announce Code Red, examine the patient for burns and document the incident",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: Code Red, a burn assessment and an incident report."},{text:"I leave the room and say nothing",correct:!1,feedback:"An incident report is needed for patient safety and for learning."},{text:"I transfer the patient to another hospital so the surgery can be continued there",correct:!1,feedback:"A full assessment is needed before any transfer."}]}],quiz:[{question:"Which extinguisher is used in the MRI room?",options:[{text:"A standard ABC extinguisher",correct:!1,feedback:"A steel extinguisher is pulled in by the magnet and becomes a projectile."},{text:"A non-magnetic extinguisher labeled for MRI",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: only non-magnetic equipment is permitted in the MRI room (Zone IV)."},{text:"Water in a steel bucket",correct:!1,feedback:"A steel bucket also becomes a projectile."},{text:"Any CO₂ extinguisher",correct:!1,feedback:"Only a non-magnetic (MR Conditional) CO₂ extinguisher may enter the room."}]},{question:"What is OEA?",options:[{text:"Fire Class D",correct:!1,feedback:"Incorrect; Class D covers combustible metals."},{text:"Air in which the oxygen concentration is above 23.5%",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: Oxygen-Enriched Atmosphere under NFPA 99."},{text:"An NFPA standard",correct:!1,feedback:"Incorrect."},{text:"A safety device",correct:!1,feedback:"Incorrect."}]},{question:"How is a fire in the lithium-ion (Li-ion) battery of a medical device put out?",options:[{text:"Only with a Class D extinguisher, because water always causes an explosion",correct:!1,feedback:"Incorrect: that applies to lithium-metal batteries. Li-ion batteries contain no metallic lithium."},{text:"With water or a water-based extinguisher and continuous cooling; Class D only for lithium-metal batteries",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: cooling prevents thermal runaway and reignition."},{text:"CO₂, and then I leave the battery alone",correct:!1,feedback:"CO₂ puts out the flames but does not cool the battery; it can reignite."},{text:"ABC, and then I do nothing more",correct:!1,feedback:"ABC does not cool the battery; thermal runaway continues."}]},{question:"During an operating room fire, what is the first step?",options:[{text:"I pick up the extinguisher",correct:!1,feedback:"Before the extinguisher, stop the oxygen flow."},{text:"Stopping the flow of oxygen and other gases to the patient",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: under ASA guidance, airway gases are stopped immediately."},{text:"Removing the patient from the room",correct:!1,feedback:"Later, if the fire is not brought under control."},{text:"Pouring water on the electrical equipment",correct:!1,feedback:"Incorrect: water on energized equipment is dangerous."}]}]},{id:4,code:"PED-04",icon:"Baby",accent:"#ec4899",category:"Medical",title:"Pediatric and Infant CPR",subtitle:"NICU, pediatric Code Blue and special procedures",summary:"Pediatric CPR differs from adult CPR in compression depth, hand technique and drug doses. Under AHA 2025, an infant is younger than 1 year (excluding newly born infants), and a child is from 1 year to puberty. The CPR sequence for infants and children is C-A-B: chest compressions, then airway and breathing. Most cardiac arrests in children result from respiratory problems, so good ventilation with oxygen is very important. Newly born infants in the delivery room and NICU are managed with the dedicated Neonatal Resuscitation Program (NRP).",facts:[{label:"Infant (under 1 year)",value:"Two thumbs or heel of the hand · about 4 cm · 100–120 per minute"},{label:"Child (1 year to puberty)",value:"One or two hands · about 5 cm · 100–120 per minute"},{label:"Newborn temperature",value:"36.5–37.5°C"},{label:"Standard",value:"AHA PALS 2025 + NRP"}],steps:[{title:"1. Recognize the arrest",detail:"The child is unresponsive, is not breathing or is only gasping. Check the pulse at the brachial artery in an infant, and at the carotid or femoral artery in a child, for no more than 10 seconds.",critical:!0},{title:"2. The C-A-B sequence and the importance of breathing",detail:"Under AHA, CPR for infants and children starts with chest compressions, followed by opening the airway and giving breaths (C-A-B). Because most cardiac arrests in children result from inadequate breathing, effective breaths with a bag-mask and oxygen must start early. Keep the infant's head in the neutral position; tilting the head too far back blocks the airway.",critical:!0},{title:"3. CPR for an infant (under 1 year)",detail:"Place both thumbs on the lower half of the breastbone and encircle the chest with your hands (two-thumb encircling technique); if you cannot encircle the chest, use the heel of one hand. The two-finger technique is no longer taught. Depth about 4 cm (one third of the chest depth). 30:2 with one rescuer, 15:2 with two rescuers.",critical:!0},{title:"4. CPR for a child (1 year to puberty)",detail:"The heel of one hand, or both hands for a larger child, on the lower half of the breastbone. Depth about 5 cm (one third of the chest depth), rate 100–120 per minute. 30:2 with one rescuer, 15:2 with two rescuers.",critical:!0},{title:"5. AED for children",detail:"For children younger than 8 years, use pediatric pads or a pediatric dose attenuator if available; if not, use adult pads. The pads must not touch each other; if the chest is small, place one on the front of the chest and one on the back (anterior-posterior). The 8-year threshold applies only to the AED dose, not to the definition of a child."},{title:"6. NICU — Neonatal resuscitation (NRP)",detail:"Keep the body temperature at 36.5–37.5°C. The first step is positive-pressure ventilation (PPV). If the heart rate is still below 60 after 30 seconds of effective ventilation: chest compressions with two thumbs encircling the chest, to about one third of the chest depth, a ratio of 3:1 (90 compressions and 30 breaths per minute) and 100% oxygen. If the heart rate is still below 60 after 60 seconds of compressions: epinephrine 0.01–0.03 mg/kg IV, repeated every 3–5 minutes if needed. During a fire, move newborns in a crib or on a cart, not in your arms."}],reference:[{heading:"Weight-based drug doses (PALS)",points:["Epinephrine: 0.01 mg/kg IV/IO every 3–5 minutes (maximum 1 mg)","Amiodarone: 5 mg/kg IV/IO for persistent VF or pVT","Atropine: 0.02 mg/kg IV/IO for vagally mediated bradycardia, after oxygen and ventilation","Adenosine: 0.1 mg/kg (maximum 6 mg) for SVT","Glucose: 0.5–1 g/kg if the blood sugar is low (hypoglycemia)"]},{heading:"Danger signs in an infant",points:["Bluish skin (cyanosis): inadequate breathing","Heart rate below 60 per minute with signs of poor perfusion: start CPR","Weakness and reduced responsiveness (lethargy)","Weak cry or no cry","Pause in breathing (apnea) longer than 20 seconds"]},{heading:"Fire in pediatric units",points:["Incubators are electrical devices and contain oxygen; the fire risk is high","Infants cannot escape on their own; horizontal evacuation comes first","Several infants can be moved together in one crib or cart","The oxygen valve is closed only after the infants have been switched to portable oxygen, and on the order of the person in charge","Move them to a safe compartment on the same floor"]}],warnings:["Do not shake an infant violently. Keep the infant's head in the neutral position; tilting the head too far back blocks the airway. AED pads must not touch each other on a small chest; in that case use anterior-posterior placement. Do not give a child the adult epinephrine dose (1 mg); the dose is weight-based: 0.01 mg/kg."],realCase:"Practical case (training example): In the NICU, a 10-day-old newborn (2.3 kg) with pneumonia stopped breathing at 03:12 and the heart rate slowed. The NICU nurse immediately started positive-pressure ventilation (PPV) with a mask and called a pediatric Code Blue. At 03:13, after 30 seconds of effective ventilation, the heart rate was still below 60; the team started chest compressions with two thumbs at a ratio of 3:1 with 100% oxygen. At 03:14 the heart rate was still below 60, so epinephrine 0.02 mg/kg IV (about 0.05 mg) was given. At 03:16 the heart rate rose above 60 and compressions were stopped; ventilation continued until the heart rate was above 100. The infant stayed in the NICU for several days and then recovered well.",caseStudy:[{prompt:"You are a nurse in the pediatric intensive care unit (PICU). At 02:40 the monitor of a 2-month-old infant shows a slow heart rate (50 per minute). The infant is cyanotic and breathing weakly, but you can feel a brachial pulse. What is the first step?",options:[{text:"I immediately start chest compressions without ventilation",correct:!1,feedback:"The infant has a pulse. Under PALS for bradycardia, first open the airway and ventilate with oxygen; if the heart rate stays below 60, compressions are started."},{text:"I open the airway, ventilate with a bag-mask and oxygen, and call for help (Code Blue)",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: for an infant with a pulse and bradycardia, oxygen and effective ventilation come first (PALS). If the infant is pulseless, CPR starts in the C-A-B sequence."},{text:"I prepare epinephrine",correct:!1,feedback:"Medications come later; oxygen and ventilation come first."},{text:"I call the elevator to move the infant",correct:!1,feedback:"Moving the infant at this point is dangerous; start treatment where the infant is."}]},{prompt:"After effective ventilation with oxygen, the infant's heart rate is still 40 per minute and there are signs of poor perfusion. What do you do?",options:[{text:"I continue ventilation only",correct:!1,feedback:"A heart rate below 60 with poor perfusion, despite good ventilation, requires CPR."},{text:"I start CPR: two thumbs encircling the chest, about 4 cm, at a ratio of 15:2 (two rescuers)",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: under PALS, a heart rate below 60 with hypoperfusion = CPR. The ratio is 15:2 with two rescuers."},{text:"I give very forceful breaths",correct:!1,feedback:"Dangerous: forceful breaths damage the lungs (barotrauma). CPR is needed."},{text:"I wait for the physician",correct:!1,feedback:"Start CPR; do not wait for the team."}]},{prompt:"The team has arrived. The infant's rhythm changes to VF and a shock is needed. There is no manual defibrillator, and the AED has only adult pads. What do you do?",options:[{text:"I do not use the AED",correct:!1,feedback:"VF requires a shock; an AED with adult pads is better than no shock at all."},{text:"I apply the adult pads: one on the front of the chest and one on the back (anterior-posterior)",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: on a small chest, anterior-posterior placement keeps the pads from touching."},{text:"I place both pads close together on the front of the chest",correct:!1,feedback:"The pads will touch each other and the shock will not be effective."},{text:"I just continue CPR",correct:!1,feedback:"In VF, CPR alone is not enough; a shock is needed."}]}],quiz:[{question:"How is CPR performed on an infant (under 1 year)?",options:[{text:"Two hands, 6 cm",correct:!1,feedback:"Far too deep for an infant."},{text:"Two thumbs encircling the chest (or the heel of one hand), about 4 cm",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: under AHA 2025; the two-finger technique is no longer taught."},{text:"One hand, 5 cm",correct:!1,feedback:"This is for children from 1 year to puberty."},{text:"CPR is not performed on infants",correct:!1,feedback:"Every infant in cardiac arrest receives CPR."}]},{question:"What is the pediatric epinephrine dose in cardiac arrest?",options:[{text:"1 mg IV",correct:!1,feedback:"This is the adult dose; it is dangerous for a child."},{text:"0.01 mg/kg IV/IO",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: weight-based, up to a maximum of 1 mg."},{text:"0.5 mg IV",correct:!1,feedback:"Far too much for an infant."},{text:"5 mg IV",correct:!1,feedback:"A lethal dose."}]},{question:"For infants and children in cardiac arrest, what is the CPR sequence under AHA?",options:[{text:"B-C-A: breaths first, then compressions",correct:!1,feedback:"Incorrect: AHA uses C-A-B for infants and children. Resuscitation of newly born infants (NRP) is different."},{text:"C-A-B: chest compressions, then airway and breathing",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: breaths follow every 30 (or 15) compressions and are very important."},{text:"Epinephrine first",correct:!1,feedback:"Medications come later."},{text:"AED first, then CPR",correct:!1,feedback:"CPR starts immediately, and the AED is used as soon as it arrives."}]},{question:"Using an AED on an infant: which pads?",options:[{text:"An AED is never used on an infant",correct:!1,feedback:"Incorrect; an AED is used when needed."},{text:"Pediatric pads or a dose attenuator; if unavailable, adult pads in the anterior-posterior position",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct."},{text:"Adult pads close together on the front of the chest",correct:!1,feedback:"The pads will touch each other."},{text:"Homemade pads",correct:!1,feedback:"Incorrect."}]}]},{id:5,code:"O2-05",icon:"Wind",accent:"#0891b2",category:"Safety",title:"Oxygen Safety",subtitle:"Cylinders, the medical gas pipeline system (MGPS) and the OEA hazard",summary:"Oxygen saves lives, but it is also dangerous. Oxygen does not burn by itself, but it makes a fire much stronger and faster and lets materials ignite at a lower temperature. A full oxygen cylinder holds very high pressure (often about 200 bar); if its valve breaks off, the cylinder behaves like a rocket. The medical gas pipeline system (MGPS) and cylinders are organized and inspected according to NFPA 99. In the operating room, oxygen, the electrocautery unit and alcohol-based prep solutions together are a major fire hazard.",facts:[{label:"Full cylinder pressure",value:"About 200 bar (2,900 psi)"},{label:"OEA threshold",value:"O₂ above 23.5% (NFPA 99)"},{label:"O₂ cylinder color",value:"White (ISO 32) / green (United States)"},{label:"MGPS standard",value:"NFPA 99"}],steps:[{title:"1. Storing and moving cylinders",detail:'Always move a cylinder on a dedicated cart, never by hand or on your shoulder. Secure cylinders to the wall with a chain or a dedicated restraint so they cannot fall. Keep empty and full cylinders apart and mark them with an "EMPTY" or "FULL" tag.',critical:!0},{title:"2. Oil and grease: two enemies of oxygen",detail:"Do not leave oil or grease on oxygen valves and fittings: it can cause ignition and explosion. Do not touch valves with hands that have cream or oil on them. In the operating room, the alcohol-based skin prep must be completely dry before the electrocautery is used.",critical:!0},{title:"3. MGPS — Medical gas pipeline system",detail:"Every unit or floor has a zone oxygen valve (zone valve box) in the corridor. Inspection and maintenance are carried out according to the hospital program and NFPA 99. During a fire, the zone valve is closed only after patients on oxygen have been switched to a portable cylinder or bag-mask, and only on the order of the clinical lead or according to the fire plan.",critical:!0},{title:"4. OEA — Recognition and reduction",detail:"If the oxygen concentration in a room is above 23.5%, it is an OEA. Oxygen can pool under drapes and in hair. Use the lowest oxygen concentration needed, allow oxygen to escape from under the drapes, and reduce oxygen near the electrocautery and other hot devices.",critical:!0},{title:"5. A fallen cylinder and a broken valve",detail:"If a cylinder falls and its valve breaks off, the gas escapes with great force and the cylinder can fly like a rocket and punch through walls. Do not try to catch it; clear the area and protect yourself. Prevention is the best approach: cylinders are always secured, and the protective cap is on the valve during transport."},{title:"6. Medical gas colors (ISO 32)",detail:"O₂: white. N₂O: blue. CO₂: gray. Helium: brown. Medical air: white and black. Colors differ in some countries (in the United States O₂ is green), so always read the gas name on the cylinder. Do not use an unlabeled cylinder: connecting the wrong gas can kill a patient."}],reference:[{heading:"Oxygen and fire: what happens?",points:["Fire burns much faster and hotter","Materials that are normally hard to burn ignite easily in an OEA","Oil or grease in contact with pressurized oxygen causes ignition and explosion","Fabric, plastic and hair become highly flammable in an OEA","Materials ignite at a lower temperature"]},{heading:"Daily staff check",points:["Cylinders are secured with a chain or a dedicated restraint","Gas pressure at the zone valve is normal","The FULL or EMPTY tag is correct","There is no oil or grease nearby","The gas alarms are working"]},{heading:"During a fire in the operating room or ICU",points:["First: rescue patients in danger; connect those on ventilators to a bag-mask or portable oxygen","Second: move patients to a safe compartment","Third: close the zone valve only after that, and on the order of the clinical lead or according to the fire plan","Full spare cylinders are ready for every patient who needs oxygen","Continuous communication between the anesthesiologist, the surgeon and the nurses"]}],warnings:["Do not leave oil or grease near oxygen, including hand creams and oil-based skin products. In the operating room, the alcohol-based prep solution must be completely dry before electrocautery is used; for ChloraPrep, at least 3 minutes on hairless skin and up to 1 hour in hair. Do not close the zone oxygen valve before patients on oxygen have been switched over."],realCase:"Practical case (training example): A storeroom worker was moving a full oxygen cylinder without a cart and without the protective cap. The cylinder fell at a doorway and its valve broke off. The gas escaped at high pressure, and the cylinder shot down the corridor like a rocket, hit a wall and injured several staff members. Had the cylinder been moved on a dedicated cart with its protective cap on, this incident would not have happened.",caseStudy:[{prompt:`You are an operating room nurse. The patient is receiving 100% oxygen. You see that the alcohol-based prep solution is still wet on the patient's skin, and the surgeon says: "Get the electrocautery ready." What do you do?`,options:[{text:"I tell the surgeon immediately and ask them to wait until the solution is completely dry (at least 3 minutes for ChloraPrep)",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: alcohol, electrocautery and oxygen together = the risk of a rapid fire. Speak up."},{text:"I only reduce the oxygen to 40%",correct:!1,feedback:"This alone is not enough: the wet solution is still flammable, and 40% is still an OEA."},{text:"I blow air on it with a fan so it dries faster",correct:!1,feedback:"Dangerous: moving air spreads the oxygen; wait for it to dry naturally."},{text:"I do nothing; this is not my job",correct:!1,feedback:"Patient safety is the whole team's responsibility. Speak up!"}]},{prompt:"The surgeon agreed and started the operation once the solution was completely dry. After a while, a small flame appears on the patient's chest. What is the first step?",options:[{text:"I pick up the CO₂ extinguisher",correct:!1,feedback:"Before the extinguisher, the oxygen flow to the patient must be stopped."},{text:"I alert the anesthesiologist to stop the O₂ flow to the patient, and I remove the burning drapes",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: the anesthesiologist controls the patient's gases. Stopping the gas and removing burning materials come before the extinguisher."},{text:"I call another nurse",correct:!1,feedback:"Time is short; the anesthesiologist is in the room and can stop the gas immediately."},{text:"I evacuate the operating room",correct:!1,feedback:"The patient is on the table; first stop the oxygen and put out the fire."}]},{prompt:"The O₂ has been stopped and saline has been poured on, but the fire is still burning. Which extinguisher is suitable in the operating room?",options:[{text:"An ABC powder extinguisher",correct:!1,feedback:"The powder contaminates the patient's wound and airway and damages the equipment."},{text:"A CO₂ extinguisher",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: ASA and ECRI recommend a CO₂ extinguisher for the operating room."},{text:"Water on the electrical equipment",correct:!1,feedback:"Dangerous: the equipment is energized."},{text:"I wait for the firefighting team and do nothing",correct:!1,feedback:"The fire is on the patient and time is very short. Use CO₂ and announce Code Red."}]}],quiz:[{question:"What does oxygen do to a fire?",options:[{text:"It puts the fire out",correct:!1,feedback:"Incorrect."},{text:"It makes the fire much stronger and faster",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: oxygen is an oxidizer."},{text:"It has no effect",correct:!1,feedback:"It has a major effect."},{text:"It cools the fire",correct:!1,feedback:"Incorrect."}]},{question:"What happens if an oxygen cylinder falls and its valve breaks off?",options:[{text:"It is a minor problem",correct:!1,feedback:"Incorrect; it is a deadly hazard."},{text:"The cylinder can fly like a rocket and kill people",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: that is why cylinders must be secured and have the protective cap on."},{text:"The pressure drops by itself and there is no danger",correct:!1,feedback:"Incorrect; high pressure produces a great force."},{text:"Only opening a window is needed",correct:!1,feedback:"Not enough; clear the area."}]},{question:"What can oil or grease in contact with pressurized oxygen cause?",options:[{text:"It has no effect",correct:!1,feedback:"Incorrect; there is a risk of ignition and explosion."},{text:"Ignition or explosion",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: oil near pressurized oxygen can ignite spontaneously."},{text:"The oil dries out",correct:!1,feedback:"Incorrect."},{text:"It changes the color of the cylinder",correct:!1,feedback:"Incorrect."}]},{question:"What is the OEA threshold under NFPA 99?",options:[{text:"O₂ above 21%",correct:!1,feedback:"Normal air contains about 20.9% oxygen."},{text:"O₂ above 23.5%",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: Oxygen-Enriched Atmosphere."},{text:"O₂ above 50%",correct:!1,feedback:"Incorrect; an OEA begins well below 50%."},{text:"O₂ below 19%",correct:!1,feedback:"That is oxygen deficiency, not an OEA."}]}]},{id:6,code:"OR-06",icon:"Stethoscope",accent:"#7c3aed",category:"Safety",title:"Fire Safety in the Operating Room (OR)",subtitle:"The fire triangle in the operating room and the fire time-out",summary:"The operating room (OR) is one of the highest-risk places for fire in a hospital, because all three sides of the fire triangle are present together: 1) oxygen or N₂O, 2) fuels (alcohol-based prep solutions, drapes, hair), 3) ignition sources (electrocautery, laser, drills). Surgical fires occur most often in head, neck and chest surgery. Under AORN and ASA guidance, the fire risk must be assessed before every operation.",facts:[{label:"Fire triangle",value:"Oxygen + fuel + ignition source"},{label:"Open oxygen near the head",value:"30% or less if the patient can tolerate it (APSF)"},{label:"High-risk procedures",value:"Head, neck, chest"},{label:"Standard",value:"AORN + ASA + ECRI"}],steps:[{title:"1. Fire time-out before surgery",detail:"Before starting, the team asks three questions: 1) How much oxygen or N₂O is in use, and is it near the surgical site? 2) What is the ignition source? 3) Where are the fuels (alcohol, drapes)? If all three are present, the procedure is high risk and needs a specific plan.",critical:!0},{title:"2. Letting alcohol-based prep dry",detail:"Before electrocautery, the alcohol-based skin prep must be completely dry according to the manufacturer's instructions: for ChloraPrep, at least 3 minutes on hairless skin and up to 1 hour in hair. Do not let the solution pool under the patient or in the drapes.",critical:!0},{title:"3. Oxygen control",detail:"For head and neck surgery, if the patient can tolerate it, open oxygen delivery (by mask or nasal cannula) should be 30% or less. If more oxygen is needed, secure the airway with an ETT or LMA. Before using an ignition source near the airway, reduce or stop the oxygen and wait about 1 minute.",critical:!0},{title:"4. Controlling ignition sources",detail:"When the electrocautery is not in use, keep it in its protective holster, not on the drapes. Laser: announce before use and move fuels away. Do not leave the tip of the fiber-optic light cable on the drapes while it is on. Do not place hot devices on flammable materials.",critical:!0},{title:"5. If a fire breaks out on the patient",detail:"1. Stop: halt the procedure and announce the fire. 2. The anesthesiologist stops the flow of O₂ and N₂O. 3. Remove the drapes and burning materials and put out the fire with saline. 4. If it persists: a CO₂ extinguisher. 5. If it is not extinguished: Code Red and evacuate the patient.",critical:!0},{title:"6. Airway fire",detail:"A fire inside the endotracheal tube (ETT). At the same time: 1) stop the flow of all gases and disconnect the circuit, 2) remove the ETT, 3) pour saline into the airway. Then: 4) ventilate by mask, and do not use supplemental oxygen until you are sure no fire remains, 5) examine the airway by bronchoscopy."}],reference:[{heading:"The fire triangle in the OR",points:["Oxidizer: O₂ (especially in an OEA) or N₂O","Fuel: alcohol, ChloraPrep, drapes, gauze, the patient's hair","Ignition source: electrocautery, laser, drill, light cable","If one of the three sides is missing, no fire can start"]},{heading:"High-risk procedures",points:["Tracheostomy: oxygen + ETT + electrocautery","Eye surgery under local anesthesia with open oxygen","ENT (head and neck): oxygen very close to the surgical site","Oral surgery: oxygen inside the mouth","Cosmetic surgery of the head and face: ChloraPrep + electrocautery"]},{heading:"Fire time-out (per AORN)",points:['Surgeon: "What is the oxygen level? What is the ignition source?"','Anesthesiologist: "Oxygen is at 21%; I am ready to stop it."','Nurse: "The prep solution is completely dry."',"Record the result on the operating room board","Repeat it for every operation, not just once"]}],warnings:["The fire time-out is the whole team's job: surgeon, anesthesiologist and nurse. If any one of them is not satisfied, the operation does not start until the risk is resolved. In an airway fire, stopping the gases and removing the ETT come first, not the extinguisher."],realCase:"Practical case (training example): During a tonsillectomy on a 7-year-old child, the 100% oxygen had not been reduced before electrocautery was used. The electrocautery ignited near the airway and the ETT caught fire (airway fire). The team did not stop the gases or remove the ETT, and brought an ABC extinguisher instead. The child suffered severe burns to the airway and mouth. The correct procedure: simultaneously stop the gases, remove the ETT and pour in saline. Lesson: reduce oxygen before electrocautery, and train regularly on the airway fire protocol.",caseStudy:[{prompt:`You are an operating room nurse. An ENT operation on a 6-year-old child is about to start. The surgeon says: "We don't need a fire time-out; this is a routine operation." What do you do?`,options:[{text:"I accept it; the surgeon is the expert",correct:!1,feedback:"Incorrect: the time-out is required (AORN), and you are also responsible for the patient's safety."},{text:'I say respectfully: "Under the National Patient Safety Goals (NPSG), the time-out is required; it only takes a few seconds."',correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: speak up. ENT procedures with oxygen and electrocautery are high risk."},{text:"Without telling the surgeon, I go straight to the nursing supervisor",correct:!1,feedback:"That may be needed later, but first speak directly with the surgeon."},{text:"I say nothing and carry on with my work",correct:!1,feedback:"Silence puts the patient at risk; you need to speak up."}]},{prompt:'The time-out has been done. Oxygen has been reduced to 30% and ChloraPrep has been applied. After 30 seconds the surgeon says: "Get the electrocautery ready." The solution is still wet. What do you do?',options:[{text:"I agree, because there is a lot of work to do",correct:!1,feedback:"There is a fire risk: ChloraPrep needs at least 3 minutes to dry completely."},{text:"I ask them to wait until the solution is completely dry (at least 3 minutes, per the manufacturer's instructions)",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: a wet alcohol-based solution is a fuel."},{text:"I set up a fan so it dries faster",correct:!1,feedback:"Dangerous: moving air spreads the oxygen."},{text:"I work quickly and do not wait",correct:!1,feedback:"There is a fire risk."}]},{prompt:"The operation has started. After 20 minutes, fire appears inside the ETT. What is the first step?",options:[{text:"I pick up the extinguisher",correct:!1,feedback:"Before the extinguisher, the gases must be stopped and the ETT removed."},{text:"At the same time, we stop the gas flow and remove the ETT, then pour saline into the airway",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: the airway fire protocol (ASA)."},{text:"I only reduce the oxygen",correct:!1,feedback:"Not enough; the gases must be stopped completely and the ETT removed."},{text:"I wait for the Code Red team",correct:!1,feedback:"There is no time; the operating room team must act immediately."}]}],quiz:[{question:"What three elements make up the fire triangle in the operating room?",options:[{text:"Heat, alcohol, fire",correct:!1,feedback:"Incorrect; fire is the result, not a side of the triangle."},{text:"Oxygen, fuels, ignition source",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: oxidizer + fuel + ignition."},{text:"Water, gas, electricity",correct:!1,feedback:"Incorrect."},{text:"Physician, nurse, equipment",correct:!1,feedback:"Incorrect."}]},{question:"How long does ChloraPrep need to dry before electrocautery?",options:[{text:"30 seconds",correct:!1,feedback:"Far too short."},{text:"At least 3 minutes on hairless skin (up to 1 hour in hair)",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: according to the manufacturer's instructions (ChloraPrep IFU)."},{text:"It does not need to dry if the oxygen is low",correct:!1,feedback:"Incorrect: a wet solution is always flammable."},{text:"There is no need to wait",correct:!1,feedback:"Incorrect."}]},{question:"In an airway fire, what is the first step?",options:[{text:"A clean agent extinguisher",correct:!1,feedback:"Later, if needed."},{text:"Stop the gases, disconnect the circuit and remove the ETT",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: the airway fire protocol."},{text:"Only reduce the FiO₂",correct:!1,feedback:"Far too little."},{text:"Start CPR",correct:!1,feedback:"Incorrect."}]},{question:"Which procedure carries a high fire risk?",options:[{text:"Appendectomy",correct:!1,feedback:"Its risk is lower."},{text:"Tracheostomy and ENT procedures",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: oxygen and electrocautery near the airway."},{text:"Hernia repair",correct:!1,feedback:"Its risk is lower."},{text:"C-section",correct:!1,feedback:"Its risk is lower."}]}]},{id:7,code:"EVAC-07",icon:"DoorOpen",accent:"#9333ea",category:"Emergency",title:"Hospital Evacuation — RACE",subtitle:"The evacuation plan for patients who cannot escape on their own",summary:"Evacuating a hospital is different from evacuating other buildings: patients on ventilators and in the NICU and ICU cannot leave immediately. Under NFPA 101 (the Life Safety Code), a hospital works at three levels: 1) defend in place, 2) horizontal evacuation to a smoke compartment on the same floor, 3) vertical evacuation to a lower floor. Full evacuation of the building is the last resort.",facts:[{label:"Level 1 — Defend in place",value:"Close the doors and stand ready"},{label:"Level 2 — Horizontal",value:"To a safe compartment on the same floor"},{label:"Level 3 — Vertical",value:"To a lower floor"},{label:"Full evacuation",value:"Last resort"}],steps:[{title:"R — Rescue (rescuing patients)",detail:"First move patients near the fire and smoke to a safe compartment. Then follow the plan: patients who can walk, those in wheelchairs, those in beds, and last ICU patients and those on ventilators, because they need a team and preparation. Never leave a patient alone.",critical:!0},{title:"A — Alarm",detail:"Push the fire alarm and call the internal emergency number; the hospital notifies 115. Code Red is announced over the public address system, the incident command system (ICS) is activated, and all staff are alerted.",critical:!0},{title:"C — Contain",detail:"Close the room doors and the smoke compartment doors. The zone oxygen valve is closed only after patients on oxygen have been switched to a portable cylinder or bag-mask, and on the order of the person in charge. The ventilation system (HVAC) is controlled by the engineering team according to the fire plan.",critical:!0},{title:"E — Extinguish or Evacuate",detail:"If the fire is small (no larger than a wastebasket), you are trained, the alarm has been raised and your exit is behind you: the appropriate extinguisher; if it does not go out at once, leave and close the door. If it is large: horizontal evacuation. Each floor is divided into two or more smoke compartments by smoke doors; patients move from the fire compartment to the safe compartment."},{title:"Horizontal evacuation — three methods",detail:`Sheet drag: the patient lies in bed and two people pull.
• Wheelchair: for patients who cannot walk far.
• Moving the bed: two people, release the wheel locks, one at the front and one at the back.`,critical:!0},{title:"Vertical evacuation — four methods",detail:`Cradle carry: for children and infants.
• Two-person seat carry: for a conscious patient who cannot walk.
• Stair chair: for bedbound patients, two people.
• Evac sled: for ICU patients and patients on ventilators, 3–4 people.`}],reference:[{heading:"Evacuation priority",points:["1. Anyone in immediate danger (near the fire and smoke)","2. Patients who can walk, with guidance","3. Patients in wheelchairs","4. Bedbound patients: by sheet or in their bed","5. ICU and ventilated patients: bag-mask and 3 or more people","6. NICU infants: several infants together in one crib or cart"]},{heading:"What must not be done",points:["Do not use the elevator; it may stop","Do not move critically ill patients without a plan and equipment","Do not leave a patient alone","Do not open windows without a reason","Do not carry personal belongings or valuables","Do not go back into the fire compartment"]},{heading:"Smoke compartments",points:["Each floor is divided into two or more compartments by smoke walls and smoke doors (NFPA 101)","Smoke doors close automatically when the fire alarm sounds","The size of each compartment is limited by NFPA 101","One compartment is the fire compartment; the other is the safe compartment","Horizontal evacuation moves toward the safe compartment"]}],warnings:["Do not use elevators during a fire: they may stop and trap people inside. Use vertical evacuation only when horizontal evacuation is not enough. Do not go back into the fire compartment without permission from the 115 team and the incident commander."],realCase:"Practical case (training example): A fire broke out in a storeroom on the first floor of a hospital. There were 140 patients on four floors. The incident command system (ICS) was activated quickly. Most patients were moved horizontally to safe compartments on the same floor, and only the patients near the storeroom were moved vertically. Four ICU patients on ventilators were moved with a bag-mask and three people for each patient. No one died. Two reasons for the success: regular ICS training, and smoke doors that worked properly.",caseStudy:[{prompt:"You are a nurse on the third-floor ICU. A Code Red has been announced in a storeroom at the end of the corridor on the same floor. You have eight patients: four in the ICU (two on ventilators) and four general patients. What do you do first?",options:[{text:"I move all the patients vertically to the first floor",correct:!1,feedback:"Horizontal evacuation comes first and is much faster; vertical only if horizontal is not enough."},{text:"Using RACE: I rescue the patients near the fire and prepare the safe compartment behind the smoke doors on the same floor, while raising the alarm",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: Rescue and horizontal evacuation. Move the patients to the safe compartment."},{text:"I leave on my own",correct:!1,feedback:"Incorrect: protecting the patients is your responsibility."},{text:"I wait for the 115 team",correct:!1,feedback:"Do not wait; the patients are in danger."}]},{prompt:"The other compartment of the third floor is safe. Two patients are on ventilators. Which patients are moved first?",options:[{text:"The patients who can walk, because they are easier",correct:!1,feedback:"Incorrect: risk is assessed first, not ease."},{text:"The ICU patients on ventilators: 3 people, a bag-mask and an evac sled",correct:!1,feedback:"That is the correct way to move them, but they do not go first if other patients are closer to the fire."},{text:"The patients closest to the smoke and fire first, then in the order set by the plan",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: Rescue begins with those in immediate danger."},{text:"All patients at the same time",correct:!1,feedback:"That is not realistic; priorities are needed."}]},{prompt:"All patients have been moved and you are in the safe compartment. The 115 team has arrived. What do you do?",options:[{text:"I go home",correct:!1,feedback:"Incorrect: you must inform the team and protect the patients."},{text:"I count the patients and brief the 115 team and the incident commander: which patients are where and what is needed",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: patient accountability and a short report to the team."},{text:"I go back to the fire compartment",correct:!1,feedback:"Going back without permission is prohibited."},{text:"I take the patients outside the building",correct:!1,feedback:"This is not necessary if the internal safe compartment is protected."}]}],quiz:[{question:"What is the first level of evacuation?",options:[{text:"Full evacuation of the hospital",correct:!1,feedback:"That is the last resort."},{text:"Defend in place",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: protect first, then move if necessary."},{text:"Vertical evacuation",correct:!1,feedback:"That is the third level."},{text:"Moving people outside the hospital",correct:!1,feedback:"That is the last resort."}]},{question:"During an evacuation, which patients are moved first?",options:[{text:"ICU patients",correct:!1,feedback:"They are in the priority order, but they do not go first unless they are in immediate danger."},{text:"Patients in immediate danger (near the fire)",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: Rescue."},{text:"Patients who can walk",correct:!1,feedback:"After those in immediate danger."},{text:"Valuables",correct:!1,feedback:"Incorrect: life comes first."}]},{question:"How is an ICU patient on a ventilator moved?",options:[{text:"One person and a wheelchair",correct:!1,feedback:"Far too few."},{text:"3 people, a bag-mask and an evac sled or the bed",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: a team is needed."},{text:"Only by elevator",correct:!1,feedback:"Elevators are prohibited during a fire."},{text:"I leave the patient where they are",correct:!1,feedback:"Incorrect."}]},{question:"What is a smoke compartment?",options:[{text:"A room where smoke collects",correct:!1,feedback:"Incorrect; it is a section designed to stop smoke from spreading."},{text:"A section of a floor separated by smoke walls and smoke doors",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: each floor has two or more compartments."},{text:"A smoke extinguisher",correct:!1,feedback:"Incorrect."},{text:"The ventilation system",correct:!1,feedback:"Incorrect."}]}]},{id:8,code:"ELEV-08",icon:"ArrowUpDown",accent:"#64748b",category:"Safety",title:"Elevator Rules in the Hospital",subtitle:"Use, entrapment in an elevator, and the ban during fires",summary:"Elevators in a hospital are important for moving patients and equipment, but they carry their own risks. Under ASME A17.1 and NFPA 101, during a fire elevators automatically go to a designated floor, and only the firefighting team (115) uses them, with a special key (firefighter service mode). Being trapped in an elevator is generally not life-threatening if people stay calm and call for help the right way; there is air inside the elevator.",facts:[{label:"Use during a fire",value:"Prohibited (NFPA 101)"},{label:"Firefighter service mode",value:"The 115 team only"},{label:"When trapped",value:"Stay calm: there is air inside"},{label:"Alarm and emergency phone",value:"Required (ASME A17.1)"}],steps:[{title:"1. Normal use",detail:"Patients on beds and in wheelchairs go first. Two staff members for every bedbound patient. Do not load more than the weight shown on the elevator capacity plate."},{title:"2. During a fire — prohibited",detail:"During a Code Red, elevators automatically go to the designated floor (Phase I recall). After that, only the 115 team can use them, with a special key (Phase II). Staff and visitors use the exit stairs.",critical:!0},{title:"3. Entrapment — first steps",detail:"Stay calm; there is air in the elevator. If a patient is with you, first check their condition (Code Blue if needed). Then press the alarm button and call for help on the elevator phone.",critical:!0},{title:"4. The elevator phone",detail:"Every elevator must have an emergency communication system (ASME A17.1) that is answered 24 hours a day: by the maintenance team or security. Say: which elevator, between which floors, how many people, and whether anyone is injured.",critical:!0},{title:"5. What is prohibited while trapped",detail:`Do not force the doors open: you could fall into the elevator shaft.
• Do not climb out through the roof hatch of the car: deadly danger.
• Do not panic; the air will not run out.
• Save your phone battery for necessary calls.`,critical:!0},{title:"6. Rescue from an elevator",detail:"Only the trained maintenance team or the 115 team, not staff or visitors. Procedure: 1) the elevator is brought to the nearest floor, 2) the door is opened with a special key, 3) the people get out safely, 4) the elevator is inspected before it is used again."}],reference:[{heading:"Phase I and Phase II",points:["Phase I (recall): during a fire, the elevator automatically goes to the designated floor","Phase II (service): only the 115 team, with a special key","For staff and visitors: completely prohibited","Use the exit stairs only"]},{heading:"Elevator buttons and devices",points:["Alarm: the alarm bell","Phone: emergency phone answered 24 hours a day","Stop: only when necessary and with instructions","Door hold: only for loading and moving beds","Fire service key: the 115 team only"]},{heading:"Moving special patients",points:["ICU and ventilated patients: a cart, a bag-mask and a spare battery","Trauma patients: a dedicated stretcher and two people","Bariatric patients: the dedicated large elevator","Wheeled stretcher: two people and the wheel locks","Wheelchair: one person is enough"]}],warnings:["Do not use the elevator during a fire: it may stop at the fire floor, or smoke may enter its shaft. Phase I recall is automatic. If you are trapped in an elevator, stay calm, use the alarm button and the elevator phone, and do not force the doors open."],realCase:"Practical case (training example): At 22:30, three staff members were in an elevator with a 75-year-old bedbound patient. The power went out and the elevator stopped between the second and third floors. One staff member immediately pressed the alarm button and alerted the maintenance team on the elevator phone. The patient had diabetes; the staff checked his condition and blood glucose every 5 minutes. The maintenance team arrived 22 minutes later and everyone got out safely. Had the doors been forced open, there would have been a risk of falling into the elevator shaft.",caseStudy:[{prompt:"You are a nurse in an elevator with an 80-year-old patient on a bed. The elevator has stopped and the power is out, but the emergency lighting (UPS) is working. What do you do first?",options:[{text:"I force the doors open",correct:!1,feedback:"Dangerous: you could fall into the elevator shaft."},{text:"I check the patient's condition: are they conscious? How are their airway, breathing and circulation (ABC)?",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: the patient's safety comes first."},{text:"I immediately press the alarm button and do not check the patient",correct:!1,feedback:"Raising the alarm is important and comes early, but a quick check of the patient comes first."},{text:"I climb out through the roof hatch of the car",correct:!1,feedback:"Deadly danger."}]},{prompt:"The patient is conscious and calm, and the ABCs are normal. What is the second step?",options:[{text:"I press the alarm button and call for help on the elevator phone",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: alarm and phone. The maintenance team answers 24 hours a day."},{text:"I do nothing and wait",correct:!1,feedback:"Without an alarm, no one knows you are trapped."},{text:"I only call a friend on my personal phone",correct:!1,feedback:"The elevator phone is connected directly to the maintenance team and is better."},{text:"I press the floor buttons",correct:!1,feedback:"The elevator does not work without power; this does no good."}]},{prompt:`The maintenance team says: "We'll be there in another 15 minutes." The patient is still stable. What do you do until the team arrives?`,options:[{text:"I climb out through the roof hatch of the car",correct:!1,feedback:"Deadly danger."},{text:"I force the doors open",correct:!1,feedback:"Incorrect: there is a risk of falling."},{text:"I check the patient every 5 minutes (vital signs), talk with them and keep them calm",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: monitoring and emotional support."},{text:"I talk on my phone nonstop until the battery runs out",correct:!1,feedback:"Save the battery; the elevator phone is more important."}]}],quiz:[{question:"During a fire, what is the rule for elevators?",options:[{text:"Use them to speed up the evacuation",correct:!1,feedback:"Incorrect: they are prohibited."},{text:"They are prohibited; only the 115 team uses them",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: NFPA 101."},{text:"Only for patients",correct:!1,feedback:"Incorrect."},{text:"Before Code Red is announced",correct:!1,feedback:"Incorrect."}]},{question:"When trapped in an elevator, what is the first step?",options:[{text:"Forcing the doors open",correct:!1,feedback:"Deadly danger."},{text:"Climbing out through the roof hatch of the car",correct:!1,feedback:"Deadly danger."},{text:"Checking the patient, then raising the alarm",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: ABC comes first."},{text:"Panicking",correct:!1,feedback:"Incorrect."}]},{question:"What is Phase I recall?",options:[{text:"Using the elevator with a special key",correct:!1,feedback:"That is Phase II."},{text:"During a fire, the elevator automatically goes to the designated floor",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: it is automatic."},{text:"Rescuing trapped people",correct:!1,feedback:"Incorrect."},{text:"Staff training",correct:!1,feedback:"Incorrect."}]},{question:"What applies to the phone inside an elevator?",options:[{text:"It is not required",correct:!1,feedback:"Incorrect: it is required (ASME A17.1)."},{text:"It is required and is answered 24 hours a day",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct."},{text:"Only during working hours",correct:!1,feedback:"Incorrect."},{text:"Only for the maintenance team",correct:!1,feedback:"It is for everyone."}]}]},{id:9,code:"SHOCK-09",icon:"Zap",accent:"#eab308",category:"Safety",title:"Electric Shock and LOTO",subtitle:"Lockout/tagout, responding to electric shock, macroshock and microshock",summary:"A hospital has hundreds of electrical devices: monitors, ventilators, pumps, defibrillators and MRI. A current of about 50 mA passing through the skin (macroshock) can be fatal. Patients with catheters or pacemaker wires that lead directly to the heart are sensitive to far smaller currents as well (microshock); that is why the permitted leakage current limit for devices connected to the heart is 10 μA. Under NFPA 99 and OSHA 1910.147, lockout/tagout (LOTO) is required for every repair of an electrical device.",facts:[{label:"Dangerous macroshock",value:"About 50 mA through the skin"},{label:"Leakage current limit",value:"10 μA (for devices connected to the heart)"},{label:"Protection in wet locations",value:"IPS or GFCI (NFPA 99)"},{label:"LOTO standard",value:"OSHA 1910.147"}],steps:[{title:"1. Macroshock and microshock",detail:"Macroshock: current passes through the skin; about 50 mA can put the heart into VF. Microshock: current reaches the heart directly through a catheter or pacemaker wire; a very small current (about 100 μA) can cause VF, so the safety limit is 10 μA. This risk exists in the ICU and CCU.",critical:!0},{title:"2. LOTO — Lockout/tagout",detail:'Six steps: 1) preparation, 2) shutting down the device, 3) isolating it from the power supply, 4) applying the lock and a "Do Not Operate" tag, 5) releasing stored energy (such as in capacitors), 6) verifying that the device does not start (try). Each worker has their own lock and key: one key, one person.',critical:!0},{title:"3. Electric shock — what not to do first",detail:"Do not touch the shocked person until the power has been cut; the current will strike you too. First cut off the power: at the circuit breaker or by pulling the plug. If you cannot, use a dry, non-conductive object (wood or plastic) to move the person away from the power source, never a metal one.",critical:!0},{title:"4. After the shock — treatment",detail:"Check ABC. If the heart has stopped: CPR and an AED. Obtain an ECG for every person who has had an electric shock. Cardiac monitoring (usually 24 hours) is for those who lost consciousness, have an abnormal ECG or cardiac symptoms, or received a high-voltage shock. Electrical burns have an entry and an exit point: examine both.",critical:!0},{title:"5. Types of electrical circuits in a hospital",detail:"Normal: regular power (220–230 V). The essential electrical system has three branches: the life safety and critical branches (ICU, OR, laboratory), whose power is restored from the generator within 10 seconds (NFPA 99 and NFPA 110, Type 10), and the equipment branch, which is connected later with an automatic delay. The most critical devices also have a UPS.",critical:!0},{title:"6. Protection in wet locations: IPS, GFCI and RCD",detail:'Under NFPA 99, wet procedure locations, such as some operating rooms, need special protection: an isolated power system (IPS), or a GFCI if a power interruption would not endanger the patient. A US-type GFCI cuts the power at about 4–6 mA; an RCD in the IEC system usually trips at 30 mA or 10 mA. Test it regularly with the "TEST" button.'}],reference:[{heading:"Signs of electrical injury",points:["Burns at the entry and exit points of the current (examine both)","Heart rhythm disturbance (arrhythmia)","Muscle breakdown (rhabdomyolysis)","Compartment syndrome","Neurological signs: altered consciousness, seizure, numbness","Hidden internal injury: blood tests (CK, troponin, K+)"]},{heading:"LOTO — equipment and procedure",points:["A dedicated LOTO lock","Tag: Do Not Operate, name and date","One key per person (not shared)","LOTO log: which device, who, and when","Try: confirm that the power has actually been cut off","Bypassing LOTO is prohibited and is a legal violation"]},{heading:"Protecting high-risk patients",points:["ICU, CCU and temporary pacemakers: risk of microshock","Medical devices must be grounded and inspected regularly","Wet locations: IPS or GFCI under NFPA 99","Defibrillators: trained staff only","MRI: the 5 gauss line; no magnetic metals nearby","Do not touch external pacemaker wires with wet hands"]}],warnings:["Do not touch a shocked person until the power has been cut off; you will be shocked too. In the ICU, a very small current through a catheter or pacemaker wire can stop the heart. Bypassing LOTO puts staff lives at risk and is a legal violation."],realCase:"Practical case (training example): A technician started repairing an IV pump in the ICU without LOTO, because he thought the power had been cut off. When he touched it, he received a 220-volt shock and his heart went into VF. A nearby staff member first cut off the power by pulling the plug (had that not been possible, a dry plastic rod could have been used to push him away from the device), then started CPR; the AED arrived within 45 seconds, and after two shocks his heart started beating again (ROSC). After several days in the ICU, he made a full recovery. Lesson: LOTO and verifying that the power is off (try), every time.",caseStudy:[{prompt:"You are an ICU nurse. An electrician has collapsed on the floor near an IV pump, with a power cable in his hand. What do you do first?",options:[{text:"I pull him away with my hands",correct:!1,feedback:"Deadly danger: the current will strike you too."},{text:"I cut off the power at the floor circuit breaker or at the plug",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: the power is always cut off first."},{text:"I immediately start CPR",correct:!1,feedback:"Until the power is cut off, touching him is dangerous."},{text:"I pour water on him to cool him down",correct:!1,feedback:"Water and electricity together: deadly danger."}]},{prompt:"The power has been cut off. The worker is unconscious. What is the second step?",options:[{text:"I check ABC, call a Code Blue and start CPR if needed",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: cardiac arrest often follows an electric shock."},{text:"The AED immediately, without an assessment",correct:!1,feedback:"First check breathing and pulse and call a Code Blue; the AED is used when it arrives."},{text:"I call the electrical staff",correct:!1,feedback:"Later; the patient comes first."},{text:"I examine the burn at the current's entry point",correct:!1,feedback:"Later; ABC comes first."}]},{prompt:"After CPR, the worker's heart started beating again (ROSC) and he is conscious. The first ECG is normal. What do you do next?",options:[{text:"I send him home",correct:!1,feedback:"Incorrect: he had a cardiac arrest and needs close monitoring."},{text:"Transfer to the ICU, cardiac monitoring (usually 24 hours) and blood tests (CK, troponin, K+)",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: after a cardiac arrest, there is a risk of arrhythmia and rhabdomyolysis."},{text:"I only examine the entry/exit burns",correct:!1,feedback:"Not enough; a full assessment is needed."},{text:"I do nothing; ROSC is enough",correct:!1,feedback:"Incorrect; the period after ROSC is very important."}]}],quiz:[{question:"Why is microshock dangerous in the ICU?",options:[{text:"Because it only occurs at 50 mA",correct:!1,feedback:"That is macroshock through the skin."},{text:"Because the current reaches the heart directly; about 100 μA can cause VF, and the safety limit is 10 μA",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: a danger for patients with catheters and pacemakers."},{text:"Because the voltage is 220 V",correct:!1,feedback:"The danger depends on the current passing through the heart, not on voltage alone."},{text:"Because the current is greater than 1 ampere",correct:!1,feedback:"Incorrect; even a very small current is enough."}]},{question:"What are the six steps of LOTO?",options:[{text:"Preparation, shutdown, isolation, lock and tag, release of stored energy, verification (try)",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: OSHA 1910.147."},{text:"Lock, tag, and start work",correct:!1,feedback:"Far too few; verification is required."},{text:"Lock only",correct:!1,feedback:"The tag and verification are also required."},{text:"Tag only",correct:!1,feedback:"The lock is also required."}]},{question:"A person has been electrocuted; what is the first step?",options:[{text:"I grab their hand",correct:!1,feedback:"The current will strike you too."},{text:"I cut off the power",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: always first."},{text:"CPR immediately",correct:!1,feedback:"After the power is cut off."},{text:"Water",correct:!1,feedback:"Dangerous."}]},{question:"After an electric shock, who needs cardiac monitoring?",options:[{text:"Everyone, for 1 hour only",correct:!1,feedback:"The monitoring period depends on each person's risk, not on a fixed number."},{text:"An ECG for everyone; monitoring (usually 24 hours) for those who lost consciousness, have an abnormal ECG or cardiac symptoms, or had a high-voltage shock",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: after a low-voltage shock with no symptoms and a normal ECG, prolonged monitoring is generally not needed."},{text:"Only 5 hours for everyone",correct:!1,feedback:"The period is set by risk, not by one fixed number for everyone."},{text:"No tests are needed, not even an ECG",correct:!1,feedback:"Incorrect: an ECG is needed for everyone who has had an electric shock."}]}]},{id:10,code:"HAZ-10",icon:"Biohazard",accent:"#16a34a",category:"Safety",title:"Hazardous Waste",subtitle:"Infectious waste, sharps, cytotoxic waste and the OSHA Bloodborne Pathogens Standard",summary:"According to WHO, about 85% of hospital waste is general, non-hazardous waste and about 15% is hazardous: infectious, toxic, chemical or radioactive. Under the OSHA Bloodborne Pathogens Standard (29 CFR 1910.1030) and WHO guidance, waste must be correctly segregated at the point of generation. Segregation errors can lead to 1) infections such as HIV, HBV and HCV, 2) legal violations, 3) environmental damage. Needlestick injuries are one of the most common hazards for health care workers.",facts:[{label:"Hazardous waste",value:"About 15% (WHO)"},{label:"General waste",value:"About 85% (WHO)"},{label:"PEP for HIV",value:"As soon as possible, up to 72 hours"},{label:"Standard",value:"OSHA 29 CFR 1910.1030"}],steps:[{title:"Waste categories and segregation",detail:`Infectious waste: blood, body fluids and laboratory cultures.
• Sharps: needles, blades and broken glass, in rigid containers.
• Cytotoxic: cancer drugs (chemotherapy).
• Pharmaceutical: expired medications.
• Chemical: formalin and mercury.
• Radioactive: under specific regulations.
• General non-hazardous waste.
Bag and container colors follow the Ministry of Health and hospital code.`,critical:!0},{title:"PPE required for the work",detail:"Gloves (nitrile, because of latex allergy), a mask (surgical or N95 depending on the risk), goggles or a face shield, a disposable protective gown and closed shoes. For cytotoxic waste: two pairs of chemotherapy-rated gloves and a chemotherapy gown.",critical:!0},{title:"Needlestick injury — immediate steps",detail:"1. Immediately wash the wound with soap and water (not alcohol or bleach). 2. Let it bleed freely, but do not squeeze hard or suck the wound. 3. Report immediately to your supervisor and to Occupational Health. 4. If HIV PEP is needed, start it as soon as possible, ideally within a few hours, and not after 72 hours; the course lasts 28 days. 5. Baseline HIV, HBV and HCV testing, then follow-up according to the Occupational Health schedule.",critical:!0},{title:"Hazardous material spill — five steps",detail:"1. Move people away from the area. 2. Put on PPE. 3. Read the material's SDS. 4. Use a spill kit (absorbent and disinfectant). 5. After cleanup, ventilate the area and write a report. For a cytotoxic spill: the pharmacy's dedicated team, following the protocol.",critical:!0},{title:"Sharps container",detail:"A rigid, puncture-resistant container. When it is three quarters full, close and replace it, and do not fill it further. Do not recap needles (recapping is prohibited). Keep the container close to the work area and at a suitable height."},{title:"Cytotoxic — Cancer drugs",detail:"These drugs are dangerous for staff as well. The USP 800 standard: two pairs of chemotherapy gloves (ASTM D6978), a chemotherapy gown and, if there is a risk of splashing or powder, eye and face protection and an N95. A dedicated chemotherapy spill kit. Every exposure must be reported."}],reference:[{heading:"Waste colors (WHO example)",points:["Colors vary from country to country; follow the code of the Ministry of Health and your hospital","Yellow: infectious waste","Yellow with the sharps symbol: rigid sharps containers","Brown: chemical and pharmaceutical waste","Cytotoxic: a specific label (often purple)","Radioactive: the radiation symbol and a dedicated container","Black: general non-hazardous waste"]},{heading:"Needlestick — first steps",points:["Wash immediately with soap and water","Do not squeeze hard or suck the wound","Report immediately to Occupational Health","HIV PEP: as soon as possible, up to 72 hours, for 28 days","Baseline HIV, HBV and HCV testing","Follow-up testing according to the Occupational Health schedule"]},{heading:"PPE by level of risk",points:["Standard: gloves, plus a mask and goggles if there is a risk of splashing","Contact: + a protective gown","Droplet: + a surgical mask","Airborne: + an N95 and a negative-pressure room","Cytotoxic: + two pairs of gloves and a chemotherapy gown","BSL-3: + an N95 or PAPR based on the risk assessment"]}],warnings:["Do not recap needles; recapping is one of the most common causes of needlestick injuries. Do not fill a sharps container more than three quarters full. A cytotoxic spill is cleaned up only by a trained team with a dedicated spill kit. Segregate waste according to the hospital color code."],realCase:"Practical case (training example): After drawing blood, a nurse recapped the needle and it went into his finger. The patient was HCV-positive. The nurse immediately washed the wound with soap and water and reported to Occupational Health within the first 45 minutes. There is no PEP for HCV, so follow-up testing was done. The first test was negative, but a later test was positive. Antiviral treatment (DAA) was given and the nurse recovered. Lesson: recapping is prohibited, and immediate reporting ensures that follow-up begins.",caseStudy:[{prompt:"You are a nurse. After drawing blood, in a hurry and out of habit, you recap the needle and it goes into your finger. What is the first step?",options:[{text:"I do nothing; the wound is small",correct:!1,feedback:"Dangerous: exposure to HIV, HBV and HCV."},{text:"I wash the wound with alcohol",correct:!1,feedback:"Soap and water are needed, not alcohol."},{text:"I immediately wash the wound with soap and water and let it bleed freely, without squeezing",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: the first step. Then report immediately, so that PEP can be started as soon as possible if needed."},{text:"I recap the needle and throw it into the sharps container",correct:!1,feedback:"Recapping is prohibited."}]},{prompt:"The wound has been washed. What is the second step?",options:[{text:"I go home",correct:!1,feedback:"Incorrect: assessment and follow-up are required."},{text:"I notify Occupational Health immediately and file a report, and HIV, HBV and HCV testing of the source patient is requested",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: an immediate assessment allows PEP to be started as soon as possible if needed."},{text:"I put a piece of cloth on it and carry on working",correct:!1,feedback:"A report is required."},{text:"I wait until next week",correct:!1,feedback:"Far too late: PEP is generally not recommended after 72 hours."}]},{prompt:"The patient is HIV-positive. Occupational Health recommends PEP. What do you do?",options:[{text:"I refuse because the wound is small",correct:!1,feedback:"There is a risk of HIV; PEP clearly reduces the risk."},{text:"I accept PEP, take the full 28-day course and attend the follow-up testing",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: PEP and follow-up according to CDC and USPHS guidance."},{text:"I take PEP for one week only",correct:!1,feedback:"The full 28-day course is required."},{text:"I stop PEP before the test results are back",correct:!1,feedback:"Incorrect: PEP continues according to the physician's decision."}]}],quiz:[{question:"When is a sharps container closed and replaced?",options:[{text:"When it is completely full",correct:!1,feedback:"Dangerous: needles stick out of the container."},{text:"When it is three quarters full",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: according to WHO guidance."},{text:"When it is half full",correct:!1,feedback:"Too early, and containers are used up unnecessarily."},{text:"Whenever you like",correct:!1,feedback:"The guidance is three quarters."}]},{question:"Why is recapping prohibited?",options:[{text:"Because it takes time",correct:!1,feedback:"The reason is the risk, not the time."},{text:"Because it is one of the most common causes of needlestick injuries",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: do not recap needles."},{text:"Because it is expensive",correct:!1,feedback:"Incorrect."},{text:"Because it makes the needle less effective",correct:!1,feedback:"Incorrect; the reason is staff safety."}]},{question:"When is HIV PEP started after an exposure?",options:[{text:"Only within the first 2 hours; after that it is of no use",correct:!1,feedback:"Incorrect: the sooner the better, but PEP can be started up to 72 hours."},{text:"As soon as possible, ideally within a few hours, and up to 72 hours",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: according to the USPHS 2025 guidelines."},{text:"After a week",correct:!1,feedback:"Far too late."},{text:"Timing does not matter",correct:!1,feedback:"Incorrect: any delay reduces its effectiveness."}]},{question:"Which gloves are used for cytotoxic drugs?",options:[{text:"One pair of latex gloves",correct:!1,feedback:"Latex causes allergies, and one pair is not enough."},{text:"Two pairs of chemotherapy gloves (ASTM D6978)",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: USP 800."},{text:"No gloves",correct:!1,feedback:"Dangerous."},{text:"Vinyl",correct:!1,feedback:"The drugs can pass through vinyl."}]}]},{id:11,code:"LAB-11",icon:"Microscope",accent:"#0d9488",category:"Safety",title:"Laboratory Safety",subtitle:"BSL levels, spills, eyewash stations and the fume hood",summary:"Hospital laboratories contain hazardous materials: chemical (acids, bases, solvents), biological (bacteria, viruses, fungi) and physical (glass, pressure, heat). Under the CDC BMBL (Biosafety in Microbiological and Biomedical Laboratories) and OSHA 29 CFR 1910.1450, there are four biosafety levels (BSL). Most clinical hospital laboratories work at BSL-2, and some work (such as TB culture) requires BSL-3. Splashes into the eyes are a major hazard, so the eyewash station must be close by and unobstructed (ANSI Z358.1).",facts:[{label:"BSL levels",value:"Four levels (BSL-1 to BSL-4)"},{label:"Clinical laboratory",value:"Usually BSL-2; some work BSL-3"},{label:"Reaching the eyewash",value:"Within 10 seconds (ANSI Z358.1)"},{label:"Eye flushing time",value:"At least 15 minutes"}],steps:[{title:"The four BSL levels",detail:"BSL-1: agents that do not cause disease in healthy people (such as E. coli K-12). BSL-2: moderate risk (Salmonella, HBV, HIV); most clinical laboratories. BSL-3: dangerous agents transmitted through the air (TB cultures and SARS-CoV-2 cultures). BSL-4: the deadliest agents (Ebola, Marburg). Each level requires specific PPE and containment equipment.",critical:!0},{title:"PPE for the work",detail:"Routine: nitrile gloves, a lab coat and safety glasses. If there is a risk of splashing: + a face shield. If aerosols may be generated: work inside a biosafety cabinet, with an N95 or PAPR based on the risk assessment. For cytotoxic drugs: two pairs of gloves and a chemotherapy gown.",critical:!0},{title:"Fume hood — correct use",detail:"Keep the front sash at the marked height, not fully open. Check the airflow indicator before starting; the face velocity of the hood is generally about 0.4–0.6 m/s. Place items at least 15 cm inside the hood. The hood is inspected and certified every year. A hood is not a storage area; it is only for working.",critical:!0},{title:"Chemical spill",detail:"1. Move people away from the area. 2. Put on PPE (splash goggles and acid-resistant gloves). 3. Read the SDS. 4. Spill kit: sodium bicarbonate for acids; citric acid for bases; absorbent material or activated carbon for solvents. 5. Mercury: only a dedicated mercury kit; do not clean it up with a broom or a vacuum. 6. Write a report and ventilate the area.",critical:!0},{title:"Eyewash and safety shower",detail:"The eyewash must be reachable within 10 seconds (about 17 meters), on the same floor and unobstructed (ANSI Z358.1). Flush the eyes with water for at least 15 minutes, both eyes if both were splashed, and hold the eyelids open. Safety shower: the same access limit and at least 15 minutes. Run the eyewash and shower every week to flush the water and to confirm that they work.",critical:!0},{title:"Broken glass and sharps",detail:"Do not pick up broken glass by hand; use tongs, or a broom and dustpan. Broken glass goes in the dedicated glass container, and Pasteur pipettes and needles go in the sharps container. Do not throw broken glass into general waste."}],reference:[{heading:"BSL in the hospital laboratory",points:["BSL-2: HIV, HBV, HCV, Salmonella","PPE: gloves, a lab coat and safety glasses","Containment equipment: Class II biosafety cabinet (BSC)","Practice: eating, drinking and applying makeup in the laboratory are prohibited","BSL-3: TB culture, Histoplasma","PPE: + respiratory protection (N95 or PAPR) and a negative-pressure room"]},{heading:"Chemical hazards",points:["Flammable: ethanol, acetone, ether","Acids: HCl, H₂SO₄, acetic acid","Bases: NaOH, KOH, ammonia","Toxic: mercury, cyanide, formaldehyde","Reactive: sodium, peroxides","Oxidizers: bleach, hydrogen peroxide"]},{heading:"Eyewash — 15 minutes",points:["Chemical splash: at least 15 minutes","Strong bases may require longer flushing; check the SDS and inform the physician","Remove contact lenses during flushing if they come out easily","Hold the eyelids open so the water reaches the whole eye","Flush both eyes if both were splashed","Then: Occupational Health and an ophthalmologist"]}],warnings:["Eating, drinking and applying makeup in the laboratory are prohibited. Do not clean up mercury with a broom or a vacuum; use only a dedicated mercury kit. Check the eyewash every week. A fume hood with its front sash fully open does not work properly; work at the marked height."],realCase:"Practical case (training example): A laboratory technician was working with 98% H₂SO₄ without safety glasses. The bottle fell and acid splashed into his eye. A colleague immediately took him to the eyewash, and his eyes were flushed with water for 15 minutes with the eyelids held open. Occupational Health arrived 20 minutes later, and an ophthalmologist examined him an hour later. After two weeks of treatment his eye recovered. Lesson: eye protection is required when working with acids, and the eyewash must be close by and unobstructed.",caseStudy:[{prompt:"You are a laboratory technician. While the centrifuge is running, a tube breaks and blood spreads inside the machine. You are wearing gloves but no protective gown. What is the first step?",options:[{text:"I open the machine right away and clean it",correct:!1,feedback:"Full PPE is required, and there is a risk of aerosols."},{text:"I stop the centrifuge and keep the lid closed, wait 30 minutes, put on full PPE and get the spill kit ready",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: under biosafety guidance, 30 minutes allows the aerosols to settle."},{text:"I open the centrifuge lid to take a look",correct:!1,feedback:"Dangerous: aerosols will spread."},{text:"I do nothing",correct:!1,feedback:"Dangerous and a violation."}]},{prompt:"After 30 minutes, the centrifuge has been opened and the spill kit is ready. What is the second step?",options:[{text:"Cleaning with bleach diluted 1:10 (or an approved disinfectant)",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: 1:10 bleach for blood spills."},{text:"Cleaning with water only",correct:!1,feedback:"Without a disinfectant, the hazard remains."},{text:"With a brush only",correct:!1,feedback:"A disinfectant is required."},{text:"I stop work and leave it for someone else",correct:!1,feedback:"You are trained and prepared; finish the job properly."}]},{prompt:"The cleanup is finished. What is the third step?",options:[{text:"I write an incident report and check myself and my PPE",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: documentation and checking yourself for exposure."},{text:"I do nothing; the cleanup is enough",correct:!1,feedback:"An incident report is required."},{text:"I leave the laboratory",correct:!1,feedback:"Not before the report."},{text:"I immediately start another tube",correct:!1,feedback:"Not before the machine has been checked."}]}],quiz:[{question:"At which BSL level does a hospital clinical laboratory generally work?",options:[{text:"BSL-1",correct:!1,feedback:"BSL-1 is for agents that pose no risk (such as E. coli K-12)."},{text:"BSL-2, and some work at BSL-3",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: HIV and HBV at BSL-2; TB culture at BSL-3."},{text:"BSL-4",correct:!1,feedback:"BSL-4 is for Ebola and exists only in specialized laboratories."},{text:"BSL-0",correct:!1,feedback:"There is no such level."}]},{question:"How long should the eyes be flushed after a chemical splash?",options:[{text:"2 minutes",correct:!1,feedback:"Far too short."},{text:"At least 15 minutes",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: ANSI Z358.1."},{text:"5 minutes",correct:!1,feedback:"Too short."},{text:"Until the pain stops",correct:!1,feedback:"Incorrect; at least 15 minutes."}]},{question:"A mercury spill: what do you use?",options:[{text:"An ordinary broom",correct:!1,feedback:"Incorrect: the mercury droplets scatter and vaporize."},{text:"A dedicated mercury kit",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: for example sulfur or zinc powder, which binds the mercury."},{text:"Soap and water",correct:!1,feedback:"It does not work."},{text:"A vacuum cleaner",correct:!1,feedback:"Dangerous: the mercury vaporizes and spreads."}]},{question:"After a tube breaks in the centrifuge, how long do you wait before opening it?",options:[{text:"I open it immediately",correct:!1,feedback:"Risk of aerosols."},{text:"30 minutes",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: the aerosols settle."},{text:"5 minutes",correct:!1,feedback:"Too short."},{text:"24 hours",correct:!1,feedback:"Far too long."}]}]},{id:12,code:"INF-12",icon:"ShieldPlus",accent:"#0e7490",category:"Safety",title:"Infection Control",subtitle:"The WHO 5 Moments, isolation precautions and preventing health care-associated infections (HAI)",summary:"Health care-associated infections (HAI) are a major risk for patients. According to the 2022 WHO report, out of every 100 patients in acute care hospitals, about 7 in high-income countries and 15 in low- and middle-income countries acquire at least one HAI; up to 70% of them can be prevented through hand hygiene and other IPC practices. Under the WHO 5 Moments and the CDC Standard Precautions, hand hygiene is the simplest and most effective measure.",facts:[{label:"HAI (WHO 2022)",value:"7 in 100 (high income) · 15 in 100 (low and middle income)"},{label:"Prevention through IPC",value:"Up to 70% (WHO)"},{label:"Standard",value:"WHO 5 Moments + CDC"},{label:"Hand hygiene time",value:"Soap and water 40–60 seconds · alcohol 20–30 seconds"}],steps:[{title:"The WHO 5 Moments for hand hygiene",detail:"1. Before touching a patient. 2. Before a clean or aseptic procedure. 3. After exposure to blood or body fluids. 4. After touching a patient. 5. After touching the patient's surroundings.",critical:!0},{title:"Hand hygiene technique",detail:"Soap and water: 40–60 seconds (when hands are visibly soiled). Alcohol-based gel: 20–30 seconds. The WHO steps: palm to palm, backs of the hands, between the fingers, backs of the fingers, thumbs, fingertips and nails, and wrists.",critical:!0},{title:"Isolation precautions",detail:"Standard: for all patients. Contact (MRSA, C. difficile, VRE): gown and gloves. Droplet (influenza, mumps): surgical mask. Airborne (TB, measles, varicella): N95 and a negative-pressure room (AIIR) with the door closed.",critical:!0},{title:"PPE — donning and doffing order",detail:"Donning: 1) gown, 2) mask or N95, 3) goggles or face shield, 4) gloves. Doffing: 1) gloves, 2) goggles, 3) gown, 4) mask or N95 (the N95 outside the room). Perform hand hygiene after removing all PPE, and also between steps if your hands become contaminated.",critical:!0},{title:"Multidrug-resistant organisms (MDRO)",detail:"MRSA: methicillin-resistant Staphylococcus. VRE: vancomycin-resistant Enterococcus. CRE: bacteria resistant to carbapenems, which are among the last-resort antibiotics. C. difficile: its spores are not killed by alcohol; soap and water are needed. Appropriate antibiotic use (antibiotic stewardship) prevents these organisms from emerging.",critical:!0},{title:"Surveillance and outbreaks",detail:"Every unit should have an infection control nurse. Four major infections: CLABSI (bloodstream, via a central line), CAUTI (urinary tract, via a catheter), VAP (lungs of patients on ventilators), SSI (surgical site). If cases rise above the usual level or several linked cases appear, notify the infection control team immediately. The hospital reports to the Duhok General Directorate of Health and the Kurdistan Region Ministry of Health according to its protocol."}],reference:[{heading:"The WHO 5 Moments — memory aid",points:["1. Before touching a patient","2. Before a clean (aseptic) procedure","3. After exposure to body fluids","4. After touching a patient","5. After touching the patient's surroundings",'Memory aid: two "befores" and three "afters"']},{heading:"Isolation signs on the door",points:["The colors and design of the signs vary from hospital to hospital; follow your hospital's IPC plan","Standard: for all patients, no sign needed","Contact: gown and gloves","Droplet: surgical mask","Airborne: N95 and a negative-pressure room (AIIR)","Protective: protecting immunocompromised patients"]},{heading:"C. difficile — special features",points:["Alcohol-based gel does not kill the spores; soap and water are needed","Clean surfaces with bleach or sporicidal disinfectants","Contact precautions according to the hospital plan (at least until 48 hours after the diarrhea stops)","A single room with its own toilet","PPE: gown and gloves at all times","Dedicated equipment for the patient (such as a stethoscope)"]}],warnings:["Alcohol-based gel does not kill C. difficile spores; soap and water are needed. The spores can survive for months on surfaces and equipment; use bleach or sporicidal disinfectants. Handwashing takes 40–60 seconds, not 10 seconds. Incorrect removal of PPE leads to contamination of the staff member themselves."],realCase:"Practical case (training example): On the oncology unit of a hospital, four patients developed C. difficile infection within two weeks. The infection control nurse investigated and found that staff were using only alcohol-based gel, not soap and water, and that surfaces were not being cleaned with a suitable disinfectant. The infection control team responded immediately: investigation, retraining, cleaning with bleach and ongoing surveillance, with a report to the health directorate according to protocol. Within a month the outbreak stopped. Lesson: staff must know the special features of C. difficile and the WHO 5 Moments well.",caseStudy:[{prompt:"You are a nurse in the ICU. A patient with suspected TB arrives, and the unit's negative-pressure room is out of service. What do you do?",options:[{text:"I put the patient in a regular room with the door open",correct:!1,feedback:"There is a risk of airborne spread throughout the unit."},{text:"I just put on an N95 and start working",correct:!1,feedback:"PPE alone is not enough; the patient needs a negative-pressure room."},{text:"I put a surgical mask on the patient, place them in a single room with the door closed, and notify infection control immediately so they can be moved to a negative-pressure room",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: TB requires an airborne infection isolation room (AIIR)."},{text:"I leave the door open so the air circulates",correct:!1,feedback:"Risk of airborne spread."}]},{prompt:"The negative-pressure room is ready. You are about to go in. What do you do before entering?",options:[{text:"I go straight in",correct:!1,feedback:"PPE comes first."},{text:"I put on PPE in the correct order: gown, N95, goggles, gloves",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: the N95 is put on before entering and a fit check is performed."},{text:"I put on the N95 after entering",correct:!1,feedback:"Incorrect: the N95 must be put on before entering."},{text:"Only a surgical mask",correct:!1,feedback:"TB requires an N95."}]},{prompt:"The work is done and you are about to leave. What is the correct order for removing PPE?",options:[{text:"Gloves, goggles, gown, then the N95 outside the room",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: the N95 is removed outside the room, followed by hand hygiene."},{text:"N95, gown, goggles, gloves",correct:!1,feedback:"The N95 is removed last, and outside the room."},{text:"I take everything off together quickly",correct:!1,feedback:"There is a risk of contaminating yourself."},{text:"I only take off the gloves; the rest is not necessary",correct:!1,feedback:"All PPE must be removed correctly."}]}],quiz:[{question:"What is the first of the WHO 5 Moments?",options:[{text:"After touching a patient",correct:!1,feedback:"That is the fourth moment."},{text:"Before touching a patient",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct."},{text:"Before eating",correct:!1,feedback:"That is not one of the WHO 5 Moments."},{text:"After leaving the hospital",correct:!1,feedback:"Incorrect."}]},{question:"For C. difficile, what are hands cleaned with?",options:[{text:"Alcohol-based gel",correct:!1,feedback:"It does not kill the spores."},{text:"Soap and water",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: rubbing and rinsing remove the spores."},{text:"Both are equally effective",correct:!1,feedback:"Alcohol-based gel is not enough here."},{text:"It is not necessary",correct:!1,feedback:"Dangerous."}]},{question:"What does airborne isolation for TB require?",options:[{text:"A surgical mask and a regular room",correct:!1,feedback:"Not enough."},{text:"An N95 and a negative-pressure room",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct."},{text:"Only an N95",correct:!1,feedback:"A dedicated room is also required."},{text:"No PPE is needed",correct:!1,feedback:"Dangerous."}]},{question:"In the PPE doffing sequence, which item is removed first?",options:[{text:"Gloves",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: gloves are the most contaminated."},{text:"Mask",correct:!1,feedback:"The mask is removed last."},{text:"Gown",correct:!1,feedback:"Later."},{text:"Goggles",correct:!1,feedback:"Later."}]}]},{id:13,code:"MED-13",icon:"Pill",accent:"#d97706",category:"Medical",title:"Medication Safety — The Five Rights",subtitle:"IPSG 3, anaphylaxis and barcode verification",summary:"Medication errors are one of the leading causes of harm in hospitals, and most of them are preventable; WHO estimates their global cost at about US$42 billion a year. Under the International Patient Safety Goals (JCI IPSG 3) and ISMP, the five rights of medication are fundamental but not sufficient: two patient identifiers, barcode scanning and an independent double-check for high-alert medications are required. Common errors: the wrong dose (such as a tenfold error), the wrong patient and the wrong route (IV instead of PO).",facts:[{label:"Cost of medication errors",value:"About US$42 billion a year (WHO)"},{label:"High-alert medications",value:"Insulin, heparin, opioids, KCl"},{label:"Standard",value:"JCI IPSG 3 + ISMP"},{label:"Anaphylaxis (adult)",value:"Epinephrine 0.3–0.5 mg IM"}],steps:[{title:"The five rights — the foundation",detail:`1. Right patient (two identifiers: name and date of birth, or medical record number, MRN). 2. Right medication. 3. Right dose. 4. Right route (IV/IM/PO/SC). 5. Right time.
• All five are required, not just some of them.`,critical:!0},{title:"Additional rights",detail:"6. Right documentation. 7. Right reason. 8. Monitoring the patient's response (right response). Many guidelines have added these rights. For high-alert medications: an independent double-check.",critical:!0},{title:"Anaphylaxis — the first step",detail:"Ask about allergies before every medication. Signs: flushed skin, red raised welts (hives), shortness of breath, high-pitched breathing (stridor), low blood pressure. Treatment: epinephrine 0.3–0.5 mg IM (1 mg/mL) immediately, into the anterolateral thigh (vastus lateralis). IV epinephrine is generally given only in cardiac arrest or by specialists. Call for help (Code Blue).",critical:!0},{title:"High-alert medications — double-check",detail:"Insulin, heparin and other anticoagulants, opioids (morphine, fentanyl), concentrated KCl and chemotherapy. All of them require an independent double-check by two staff members and barcode scanning.",critical:!0},{title:"Barcode medication administration (BCMA)",detail:"BCMA: barcode medication administration. Scan: 1) the patient's wristband, 2) the medication, 3) the system confirms the match. Studies have shown that BCMA clearly reduces medication administration errors. Bypassing BCMA is not allowed without an official, documented reason.",critical:!0},{title:"Look-alike, sound-alike medications (LASA)",detail:"Medications whose names or appearance are similar (look-alike, sound-alike): hydromorphone and morphine, heparin and Hespan, vincristine and vinblastine, Humalog and Humulin. ISMP recommends tall man lettering, such as hydroMORPHONE and morphine. Do not work from memory: read, verify, scan."}],reference:[{heading:"Rights checklist",points:["R1: two identifiers (not the room or bed number)","R2: the medication matches the medication administration record (MAR)","R3: the dose matches (mL, mg, units)","R4: the route matches (IV/IM/PO)","R5: the time matches (hour and frequency)","R6: documentation in the MAR","R7: allergy check","R8: monitoring the response after administration"]},{heading:"Anaphylaxis protocol",points:["First: epinephrine 0.3–0.5 mg IM into the thigh","Code Blue and call for help","IV access and intravenous fluids","ABC and oxygen","Antihistamines and steroids only as adjunct therapy, not in place of epinephrine","Repeat IM epinephrine every 5–15 minutes if needed","Observation for several hours depending on the severity"]},{heading:"Common errors",points:["1. Wrong dose (such as a tenfold error)","2. Wrong patient","3. Wrong route (IV instead of PO)","4. Wrong time","5. Drug interactions","6. Missed allergy","7. Mixing up LASA medications","8. Verbal order error","9. Dose calculation error","10. Bypassing BCMA"]}],warnings:["Do not accept a verbal order without read-back: 1) write it down, 2) read it back to the physician, 3) get their confirmation. Concentrated KCl must not be stocked on patient units; only premixed solutions. KCl is never given by IV push: it is lethal. Bypassing the barcode without an official reason is prohibited."],realCase:"Practical case (training example): A nurse had two patients: one needed IV KCl as a slow infusion (per protocol, generally no more than 10 mEq per hour through a peripheral vein), and the other needed only saline. In a hurry and without scanning the barcode, concentrated KCl was given by IV push. Shortly afterward the patient's heart went into VF. Code Blue, CPR and an AED; 4 minutes later the heart started beating again (ROSC). The investigation found: BCMA had been bypassed, there was no double-check, and concentrated KCl was stocked on the unit. After that, concentrated KCl was removed from the units and BCMA became a hard stop.",caseStudy:[{prompt:'You are an ICU nurse. The physician says verbally: "Give KCl 20 mEq IV to the patient in Room 302." What do you do?',options:[{text:"I give the medication right away; the physician is the expert",correct:!1,feedback:"Incorrect: a verbal order requires read-back (JCI)."},{text:'I write it down and read it back: "KCl 20 mEq IV as an infusion for the patient in Room 302, correct?" and ask for the order to be entered in the system',correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: write it down, read it back and confirm."},{text:"I refuse; all verbal orders are prohibited",correct:!1,feedback:"Incorrect: verbal orders are not prohibited, but read-back is required."},{text:"I do nothing until the order appears in the system",correct:!1,feedback:"A delay can harm the patient; do a read-back."}]},{prompt:"The physician has confirmed. You go to the medication cabinet (Pyxis). There is no premixed KCl 20 mEq, only concentrated KCl 40 mEq. What do you do?",options:[{text:"I give half of the concentrated KCl by IV push",correct:!1,feedback:"Incorrect: concentrated KCl is never given by IV push."},{text:"I call the pharmacy and ask for premixed KCl",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: only premixed solutions, as a slow infusion."},{text:"I give it by IV push because it is faster",correct:!1,feedback:"Lethal: VF."},{text:"I give it by mouth (PO)",correct:!1,feedback:"The route cannot be changed without a new order."}]},{prompt:"The pharmacy has sent premixed KCl 20 mEq/100 mL. There are two patients in Room 302: one is awake and the other is asleep. What is the first step?",options:[{text:"I start the infusion; it is the right room",correct:!1,feedback:"A room number is not an identifier; two identifiers are required."},{text:"I scan the wristband and confirm two identifiers (name and date of birth)",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: IPSG 1 and BCMA."},{text:"I ask the other patient which one needs the medication",correct:!1,feedback:"That is not proper identification."},{text:"I only read the MAR",correct:!1,feedback:"The wristband and barcode are also required."}]}],quiz:[{question:"IV KCl: what is never done?",options:[{text:"Giving a premixed solution",correct:!1,feedback:"A premixed solution as a slow infusion is the correct method."},{text:"IV push (especially concentrated KCl)",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: it is lethal."},{text:"A slow infusion",correct:!1,feedback:"A slow infusion of a premixed solution according to protocol is safe."},{text:"PO",correct:!1,feedback:"The oral route can be safe if there is an order."}]},{question:"In anaphylaxis, what is the first drug?",options:[{text:"Benadryl PO",correct:!1,feedback:"Far too slow; epinephrine comes first."},{text:"Epinephrine 0.3–0.5 mg IM",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: into the thigh (vastus lateralis)."},{text:"IV fluids",correct:!1,feedback:"Later; epinephrine comes first."},{text:"O₂ only",correct:!1,feedback:"Without epinephrine, the patient's life is in danger."}]},{question:"Which two identifiers are required?",options:[{text:"Name only",correct:!1,feedback:"One identifier is not enough."},{text:"Name and date of birth, or MRN",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: IPSG 1."},{text:"Room number",correct:!1,feedback:"A room number is not an identifier."},{text:"Bed number",correct:!1,feedback:"Dangerous; patients get moved."}]},{question:"Verbal orders: is read-back needed?",options:[{text:"It is not needed",correct:!1,feedback:"Incorrect."},{text:"It is required: write down, read back and confirm (JCI)",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct."},{text:"Only for high-alert medications",correct:!1,feedback:"It is required for all verbal orders."},{text:"Only in the ICU",correct:!1,feedback:"It is required on all units."}]}]},{id:14,code:"ICS-14",icon:"ClipboardList",accent:"#a855f7",category:"Emergency",title:"Incident Command System (ICS) and Hospital Codes",subtitle:"HICS, color codes and casualty triage",summary:"The incident command system (ICS) is a standardized approach to managing disasters. Hospitals use HICS (Hospital Incident Command System). Color codes are used for rapid internal alerts, but the meaning of some codes differs from one hospital to another, so every staff member must know their own hospital's code list. Under the Joint Commission and NIMS, a hospital must have an emergency operations plan (EOP) and conduct emergency exercises at least twice a year.",facts:[{label:"Standard",value:"HICS + NIMS"},{label:"Emergency exercises",value:"At least 2 times a year"},{label:"Hospital codes",value:"According to your own hospital's list"},{label:"Triage",value:"START (adults) + JumpSTART (children)"}],steps:[{title:"HICS — five main sections",detail:"1. Command: the incident commander. 2. Operations: patient care, infrastructure and security. 3. Planning: situation status, documentation and resources. 4. Logistics: supplies, communications and food. 5. Finance/Administration: time records, procurement and compensation. Each section has a section chief.",critical:!0},{title:"Color codes (example)",detail:`Code Red: fire.
• Code Blue: cardiac or respiratory arrest.
• Code Black: bomb threat.
• Code Orange: hazardous materials (HazMat).
• Code Yellow: disaster or mass casualty incident (MCI).
• Code Green: evacuation.
• Code Gray or Violet: violent person.
• Code Pink: infant abduction.
• Code Brown: severe weather.
The meaning of codes varies between hospitals; follow your own hospital's list.`,critical:!0},{title:"START triage — for adults",detail:`Simple Triage And Rapid Treatment. Four categories:
• Green (minor): can walk.
• Yellow (delayed): serious injury, but can wait.
• Red (immediate): life-threatening, immediate treatment.
• Black (expectant/deceased): dead or no chance of survival.
Assess each casualty in about 30–60 seconds. If not breathing: open the airway; if breathing starts: red; if not: black. RPM: respirations above 30 per minute = red; no radial pulse or capillary refill longer than 2 seconds = red; cannot follow simple commands = red; otherwise: yellow.`,critical:!0},{title:"JumpSTART — for children (about 1–8 years)",detail:"If the child is not breathing: open the airway; if breathing starts: red. If still not breathing: check for a peripheral pulse; if there is none: black. If there is a pulse: give 5 rescue breaths; if breathing starts: red; if not: black. If the child is breathing: a respiratory rate below 15 or above 45 per minute = red. If it is between 15 and 45, check the peripheral pulse: absent = red. Then AVPU: inappropriate P or U = red; A, V or appropriate P = yellow.",critical:!0},{title:"EOP — Emergency operations plan",detail:"Six critical areas: 1) communications, 2) resources and supplies, 3) safety and security, 4) staff roles, 5) utilities (water, power, gas), 6) patient care. A hazard vulnerability analysis is carried out every year. At least two emergency exercises a year, plus separate fire drills under NFPA 101.",critical:!0},{title:"Communication during a major disaster",detail:"First: the hospital telephone system and pagers. Second: mobile phones and radios. Third: staff runners who carry messages. Backup: satellite phone. NIMS recommends the use of plain language; many hospitals issue alerts in plain language instead of codes."}],reference:[{heading:"Example hospital codes",points:["Red: fire","Blue: cardiac or respiratory arrest","Black: bomb threat","Orange: hazardous materials","Yellow: disaster or MCI","Green: evacuation","Gray: violent person","Violet: violent person (in some hospitals)","Pink: infant abduction","Brown: severe weather","Silver: person with a weapon (in some hospitals)","The meaning of codes varies; know your own hospital's list"]},{heading:"START triage categories",points:["Green: minor injuries, can walk","Yellow: serious but stable, can wait","Red: life-threatening, immediate","Black: deceased or expectant","Attach the triage tag to the casualty","Repeat triage (re-triage) regularly and whenever the condition changes"]},{heading:"HICS roles",points:["Incident Commander","Public Information Officer (PIO)","Liaison Officer","Safety Officer","Operations Section Chief","Planning Section Chief","Logistics Section Chief","Finance/Administration Section Chief"]}],warnings:["Outside the hospital and with the public, use plain language, not codes. Internally, use only the codes on your own hospital's list. JumpSTART is for children aged about 1–8 years; START is for adults. In JumpSTART, a respiratory rate below 15 or above 45 = red (immediate), not black. Do not remove the triage tag from a casualty."],realCase:"Practical case (training example for drills): A car explosion occurred near a hospital, and 42 casualties arrived within the first 10 minutes. Code Yellow was announced and HICS was activated; an incident commander was designated within the first 2 minutes. START triage in the emergency department: 8 red, 12 yellow, 15 green and 7 black. The hospital suspended non-urgent surgery, discharged stable patients and opened additional beds (surge capacity). 35 casualties survived. Lesson: regular HICS exercises and continuous re-triage.",caseStudy:[{prompt:"You are an emergency department (ER) nurse. An alert comes in: a car explosion, with 30 or more casualties on their way. What do you do first?",options:[{text:"I immediately start treating the first casualty who arrives",correct:!1,feedback:"Without triage and organization, resources are used wrongly."},{text:"I announce Code Yellow, ICS is activated, and I get the START triage tags ready",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: the mass casualty incident (MCI) protocol."},{text:"I close the door until the team has assembled",correct:!1,feedback:"The casualties are left without treatment."},{text:"I wait for the physician",correct:!1,feedback:"There is no time; you have to act."}]},{prompt:"First casualty: 35 years old, conscious, able to walk, minor bleeding from the shoulder, respiratory rate 20 per minute. Which color?",options:[{text:"Red",correct:!1,feedback:"Conscious and able to walk; not red."},{text:"Green (walking wounded)",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: in START, anyone who can walk is green at first and is then re-triaged."},{text:"Yellow",correct:!1,feedback:"A person who can walk is green at first, not yellow."},{text:"Black",correct:!1,feedback:"Incorrect: the casualty is conscious."}]},{prompt:"Second casualty: 22 years old, unconscious and not breathing. You have opened the airway (repositioning), but there is still no breathing. Which color in START?",options:[{text:"Red (immediate)",correct:!1,feedback:"In START, a casualty who starts breathing after the airway is opened becomes red; but this casualty is still not breathing."},{text:"Black (expectant/deceased)",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: in an MCI, if there is no breathing after the airway is opened, the casualty is black. CPR is not performed during triage, so that resources remain for those who have a chance of survival."},{text:"Yellow",correct:!1,feedback:"Incorrect."},{text:"Immediate CPR",correct:!1,feedback:"In an MCI, triage comes first; CPR takes resources away from other casualties."}]}],quiz:[{question:"What does Code Pink generally mean?",options:[{text:"Fire",correct:!1,feedback:"That is Code Red."},{text:"Infant abduction",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct."},{text:"Cardiac arrest",correct:!1,feedback:"That is Code Blue."},{text:"Flood",correct:!1,feedback:"Incorrect."}]},{question:"In START triage, what does black mean?",options:[{text:"Minor injuries",correct:!1,feedback:"That is green."},{text:"Deceased or expectant",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct."},{text:"Life-threatening",correct:!1,feedback:"That is red."},{text:"Stable",correct:!1,feedback:"That is yellow."}]},{question:"Which is the principal section of HICS?",options:[{text:"Operations",correct:!1,feedback:"It falls under Command."},{text:"Command",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: the incident commander is at the top."},{text:"Logistics",correct:!1,feedback:"It falls under Command."},{text:"Finance",correct:!1,feedback:"It falls under Command."}]},{question:"In JumpSTART, a child is breathing 50 times per minute. Which color?",options:[{text:"Black",correct:!1,feedback:"Incorrect: black is only for a child who is still not breathing after the airway is opened and 5 rescue breaths are given, or who has no pulse."},{text:"Red (immediate)",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: a respiratory rate below 15 or above 45 = red."},{text:"Yellow",correct:!1,feedback:"Yellow only if the respiratory rate is between 15 and 45, a pulse is present and AVPU is appropriate."},{text:"Green",correct:!1,feedback:"Green is for those who can walk."}]}]},{id:15,code:"QUAKE-15",icon:"Activity",accent:"#a16207",category:"Disaster",title:"Earthquakes in the Hospital",subtitle:"Drop, Cover, Hold On, protecting patients and inspecting the building",summary:"Earthquakes are a major hazard for hospitals: bedbound patients cannot escape immediately, and equipment, gas cylinders and heavy devices can fall. Under FEMA and the WHO Hospital Safety Index, hospitals must be built and prepared so that they keep functioning after an earthquake as well. The Kurdistan Region lies on the Zagros belt, an active earthquake zone. This lesson explains what to do during the shaking, how to protect patients and how the building is inspected after the earthquake.",facts:[{label:"Kurdistan's seismic zone",value:"The Zagros belt"},{label:"During the shaking",value:"Drop, Cover, Hold On"},{label:"Standard",value:"FEMA + WHO Hospital Safety Index"},{label:"Building inspection",value:"ATC-20 (green · yellow · red)"}],steps:[{title:"1. During the shaking — Drop, Cover, Hold On",detail:"Drop: immediately get down on your hands and knees. Cover: protect your head and neck under a sturdy table, away from windows and heavy equipment. Hold On: hold on to the table and stay there until the shaking stops. Do not run to the door, outside or to the elevator.",critical:!0},{title:"2. Bedbound patients",detail:"Lock the bed wheels. The patient stays in bed and protects their head with a pillow. If there is time, move objects that could fall away from the patient. ICU patients and patients on ventilators stay where they are and are protected with pillows. Do not move patients out during the shaking; the building is still moving.",critical:!0},{title:"3. After the shaking — the first five minutes",detail:"1. Check yourself and your colleagues: is anyone injured? 2. Count and check the patients. 3. Check the ventilators and the oxygen supply. 4. Check the gas lines; if you smell gas or hear it escaping, only trained, designated staff shut the gas valve, and only if they can reach it safely, without entering the area where the gas has collected and without any spark; otherwise keep everyone away from that area and call 115. 5. Notify engineering and the ICS.",critical:!0},{title:"4. Building inspection — rapid damage assessment",detail:`ATC-20 has three placard categories:
• Green (inspected): inspected and safe to use.
• Yellow (restricted use): limited use.
• Red (unsafe): not safe; do not enter.
Only engineers or trained, designated inspectors post the placards. Do not go back into a damaged building without an inspection.`,critical:!0},{title:"5. Aftershocks",detail:"After the main earthquake, aftershocks occur most often in the early period and can continue for days or weeks; they can bring down weakened buildings. Keep patients in safe areas until the engineering inspection. With every aftershock, Drop, Cover, Hold On again.",critical:!0},{title:"6. Evacuating the building",detail:"Only if the building receives a red placard or engineering requests it. Prepare the patient list before evacuating. Horizontal evacuation before vertical. 3 people for every ICU or ventilated patient. Do not carry valuables. The assembly point must be away from buildings, power lines and walls."}],reference:[{heading:"Drop, Cover, Hold On",points:["DROP: immediately onto your knees","COVER: under a sturdy table, protect your head and neck","HOLD ON: hold on to the table and stay there","Stay until the shaking stops","Do not run to the door","Do not use the elevator","Do not stay near windows"]},{heading:"ATC-20 placards",points:["Green: inspected, can be used","Yellow: restricted use, limited entry","Red: unsafe, do not enter","Do not go back into a building without a placard","Only engineers or designated inspectors post the placards","After every strong aftershock, the inspection is repeated"]},{heading:"Weak points in a hospital",points:["Nonstructural elements: gas cylinders, CT, MRI and cabinets","Falling objects: equipment and supplies on shelves","Breaking glass: windows","Power outage: generators and UPS","Water outage: reserve tanks","Communications: radios and satellite phones"]}],warnings:["Do not use elevators during or after the shaking. Do not go back into a damaged building without an engineering inspection and placard. Aftershocks can bring down weakened buildings; keep patients in safe areas. The gas valve is shut only if there is a smell of gas or a leaking sound, only by trained, designated staff and only if it can be reached safely; do not cut off the oxygen supply to patients who need it."],realCase:"On February 6, 2023, two strong earthquakes (Mw 7.8 and Mw 7.5, according to the USGS) struck southern Türkiye and northern Syria. According to news reports, in Iskenderun, part of the state hospital collapsed and people died. Lessons for Kurdistan: build hospitals to seismic codes, anchor equipment and cylinders to the walls, and train regularly in Drop, Cover, Hold On and evacuation.",caseStudy:[{prompt:"You are an ICU nurse. At 02:47 at night the ground shakes violently. There are two patients on ventilators and four other patients. What do you do first?",options:[{text:"I run to the door",correct:!1,feedback:"Incorrect: you may fall or be struck by falling objects."},{text:"Drop, Cover, Hold On under a sturdy table, and if I am close by, I lock the bed wheels",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: your own safety first; an injured staff member cannot protect patients."},{text:"I take the patients outside immediately",correct:!1,feedback:"Dangerous: the building is still shaking."},{text:"I close the room door and stand in front of it",correct:!1,feedback:"In front of the door is not a safe place; take cover under a sturdy table."}]},{prompt:"The shaking stopped after 45 seconds. You are back on your feet. What is the first step?",options:[{text:"I leave right away on my own",correct:!1,feedback:"Incorrect: the patients need you, and leaving without an inspection is dangerous."},{text:"I check myself and the patients, check the ventilators and the oxygen, and if I smell gas I report it so that trained staff shut the gas valve only if it is safe to reach",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: check yourself, the patients and the equipment."},{text:"I take the patients to the elevator",correct:!1,feedback:"Elevators are not used after an earthquake."},{text:"I stay where I am and do nothing",correct:!1,feedback:"You need to check the patients and be ready for aftershocks."}]},{prompt:"The check is complete. A patient near the door has been hit by a falling object and has a neck injury. What do you do?",options:[{text:"I lift him immediately and put him back in bed",correct:!1,feedback:"Before moving him, spinal precautions (protecting the neck and back) are needed."},{text:"I call a Code Blue or the trauma team, protect the neck and back, and notify engineering to inspect the building",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: treating the injured and building safety together."},{text:"I leave on my own",correct:!1,feedback:"Incorrect: the patient needs you."},{text:"I take him to the emergency department by elevator",correct:!1,feedback:"Elevators are not used after an earthquake; use the stairs."}]}],quiz:[{question:"What is Drop, Cover, Hold On?",options:[{text:"Running to the door",correct:!1,feedback:"Dangerous."},{text:"Getting down on your knees, under a sturdy table, and holding on to the table",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: the best protection during the shaking."},{text:"Standing near a window",correct:!1,feedback:"Danger from glass."},{text:"Running to the elevator",correct:!1,feedback:"Incorrect."}]},{question:"What does a red placard (ATC-20) mean?",options:[{text:"It is safe",correct:!1,feedback:"That is green."},{text:"Unsafe: do not enter",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct."},{text:"Restricted entry",correct:!1,feedback:"That is yellow."},{text:"The building has completely collapsed",correct:!1,feedback:"Not necessarily; red means it is not safe to enter."}]},{question:"When do aftershocks occur?",options:[{text:"Never",correct:!1,feedback:"Incorrect: they always follow strong earthquakes."},{text:"Mostly soon after the main earthquake, and they can continue for days or weeks",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: that is why you must not go back into a building without an inspection."},{text:"Only in the first 5 minutes",correct:!1,feedback:"Incorrect: they can also occur much later."},{text:"Only after a week",correct:!1,feedback:"Incorrect: they occur most often in the early period."}]},{question:"After the earthquake, what do you do with gas lines and gas equipment?",options:[{text:"No check is needed",correct:!1,feedback:"There is a risk of a gas leak."},{text:"I check them; if there is a smell of gas or a leaking sound, trained staff shut the gas valve only if it is safe to reach",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct."},{text:"I shut all the valves immediately, including the patients' oxygen",correct:!1,feedback:"Incorrect: cutting off oxygen puts patients at risk."},{text:"Only if a device is broken",correct:!1,feedback:"Check all the lines and equipment."}]}]},{id:16,code:"COLD-16",icon:"Snowflake",accent:"#0ea5e9",category:"Safety",title:"Winter Hazards",subtitle:"Hypothermia, frostbite, the backup generator and slip prevention",summary:"Winter in Kurdistan brings specific hazards for hospitals: snow and ice, slips, power outages, hypothermia (especially in the elderly) and frostbite. Hypothermia is generally classified into three levels: mild (32–35°C), moderate (28–32°C) and severe (below 28°C). Generators tested according to NFPA 110, reserve fuel and extra blankets are the foundations of protection.",facts:[{label:"Mild hypothermia",value:"32–35°C"},{label:"Moderate hypothermia",value:"28–32°C"},{label:"Severe hypothermia",value:"Below 28°C"},{label:"Power restored by generator",value:"Within 10 seconds (NFPA 110, Type 10)"}],steps:[{title:"Hypothermia — recognition",detail:"Mild (32–35°C): shivering, confusion and slurred speech. Moderate (28–32°C): shivering stops, the heart rate and breathing slow, and the pupils dilate. Severe (below 28°C): unconsciousness, heart rhythm disturbances (J wave/Osborn wave), VF or asystole. Core temperature is measured rectally or esophageally, not on the skin.",critical:!0},{title:"Rewarming — three methods",detail:"Passive (mild): blankets, a warm room and removing wet clothing. Active external (moderate): forced warm air (Bair Hugger) and warmed IV fluids (38–42°C). Active core (severe): warm, humidified oxygen, warmed IV fluids, and ECMO or CPB for unstable patients or those in cardiac arrest. Move the patient gently and keep them horizontal; rough handling can trigger VF.",critical:!0},{title:"Frostbite — four degrees",detail:"1st degree: white or yellow skin and numbness. 2nd degree: blisters with clear fluid. 3rd degree: blood-filled blisters and deeper damage. 4th degree: damage reaches muscle and bone (tissue death). Rewarming: water at 37–39°C, for about 15–30 minutes, until the skin is soft. Do not use: hot water, direct heat (fire or a heater), or massage. If there is a risk of refreezing, do not rewarm.",critical:!0},{title:"Backup generator — NFPA 110",detail:"Type 10: power is restored within 10 seconds. Class: the minimum running time in hours without refueling; hospitals use Class X (per NFPA 99), often 96 hours in earthquake zones. Level 1: for life safety and critical loads (ICU, OR, laboratory). Testing: weekly visual inspection, monthly run under load for at least 30 minutes, and a 4-hour run under load at least once every 36 months.",critical:!0},{title:"Preventing slips and falls",detail:'Clear snow and ice from entrances and walkways regularly. Spread salt or calcium chloride on walkways. Nonslip shoes for staff. Winter tires for ambulances. "Wet Floor" signs in wet areas.',critical:!0},{title:"Special winter situations",detail:"Carbon monoxide (CO) poisoning (from generators or stoves): headache, confusion and nausea. SpO₂ is not reliable; CO-oximetry is required. Treatment: 100% O₂, and hyperbaric oxygen in severe cases at the specialist's discretion. Winter is the season of rising influenza and RSV in children; have a surge plan ready for the emergency department."}],reference:[{heading:"Hypothermia stages and treatment",points:["Mild (32–35): passive rewarming, and warm drinks if the patient is alert and can swallow safely","Moderate (28–32): Bair Hugger and warmed IV fluids","Severe (below 28): active core rewarming and ECMO/CPB","Move the patient gently and keep them horizontal",'"Not dead until warm and dead": death is not declared on the basis of cold alone',"Continue CPR during rewarming (generally until about 32°C) before deciding to stop"]},{heading:"Recognizing CO poisoning",points:["Headache, dizziness and confusion","Cherry-red lips are a late and rare sign","SpO₂ can appear falsely normal","The COHb level is measured by CO-oximetry","Treatment: 100% O₂ until the symptoms resolve (usually several hours)","Hyperbaric oxygen for severe cases, pregnant women or neurological symptoms, at the specialist's discretion"]},{heading:"Generator testing (NFPA 110)",points:["Weekly: visual inspection of the generator and fuel","Monthly: run under load for at least 30 minutes","At least every 36 months: a 4-hour run under load","Fuel: according to the generator's class (for example, 96 hours)","Power restoration: within 10 seconds (Type 10)","Batteries: starting batteries and UPS"]}],warnings:['"Not dead until warm and dead": a patient with severe hypothermia can be in asystole and still be saved; continue CPR and rewarming before deciding to stop. Do not rewarm frostbite with hot water; use only 37–39°C. Test the generator regularly and under load; an untested generator may fail when it is needed.'],realCase:"Practical case (training example): Heavy snow cut off power to the city for 48 hours. The hospital generator started within 10 seconds (Type 10), but the hospital's fuel was enough for only 12 hours, and a new tanker had difficulty getting through. During this time many elderly patients arrived with mild hypothermia and were treated with blankets, Bair Huggers and warmed fluids. Two staff members at the entrance developed mild frostbite. Lessons: reserve fuel according to the generator's class and a refueling plan, Bair Huggers on every unit, and winter footwear and clothing for staff.",caseStudy:[{prompt:"You are an emergency department nurse. The city's power is out because of snow and the generator is running. A 72-year-old patient arrives who spent 45 minutes in the street. He is shivering violently, is confused and has a GCS of 13. What is the first step?",options:[{text:"Immediate active core rewarming",correct:!1,feedback:"Too aggressive; for mild hypothermia, passive and active external rewarming are enough."},{text:"I measure the core temperature (rectal/esophageal), remove wet clothing and start passive rewarming",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: shivering and confusion indicate mild hypothermia; a Bair Hugger and warmed fluids can be added."},{text:"I just give him a blanket",correct:!1,feedback:"Measuring the core temperature is needed to determine the level."},{text:"I give him hot water to drink",correct:!1,feedback:"Dangerous: he is confused (risk of aspiration), and hot water burns."}]},{prompt:"The core temperature is 27.5°C, the GCS is 6 and the pulse is weak. What is the next step?",options:[{text:"Passive rewarming and blankets",correct:!1,feedback:"Not enough for severe hypothermia; active core rewarming is needed."},{text:"Active core rewarming: warm, humidified oxygen, warmed IV fluids, and preparing ECMO/CPB",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: severe hypothermia; move the patient gently."},{text:"I start CPR immediately even though there is a pulse",correct:!1,feedback:"CPR is not performed on a patient who has a pulse; active core rewarming is needed."},{text:"I pronounce him dead",correct:!1,feedback:'"Not dead until warm and dead": incorrect.'}]},{prompt:"Active core rewarming has been started. After 3 hours, the core temperature is 29°C. The ECG shows VF and the patient is pulseless. What is the correct step?",options:[{text:"I give no shocks until 32°C",correct:!1,feedback:"Incorrect: shocks must not be withheld until 32°C; AHA recommends defibrillation alongside rewarming."},{text:"I continue CPR and deliver shocks (per the ACLS algorithm; per ERC, up to 3 shocks below 30°C), with active rewarming and ECMO if available",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: a cold heart may not respond to shocks until the temperature rises above 30°C, so rewarming is the most important treatment."},{text:"I only give medications and never deliver a shock",correct:!1,feedback:"Incorrect: VF requires a shock."},{text:"I pronounce him dead",correct:!1,feedback:"Incorrect: he can still be saved."}]}],quiz:[{question:"At what temperature is hypothermia severe?",options:[{text:"Below 35°C",correct:!1,feedback:"That is the threshold for mild hypothermia."},{text:"Below 28°C",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct."},{text:"Below 32°C",correct:!1,feedback:"That is the threshold for moderate hypothermia."},{text:"Below 20°C",correct:!1,feedback:"Incorrect."}]},{question:"What water temperature is used to rewarm frostbite?",options:[{text:"25°C",correct:!1,feedback:"Far too cold."},{text:"37–39°C",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: for about 15–30 minutes, until the skin is soft (Wilderness Medical Society)."},{text:"50°C",correct:!1,feedback:"Far too hot; it burns the skin."},{text:"Boiling water",correct:!1,feedback:"Dangerous: burns."}]},{question:"Under NFPA 110, within what time is power restored by the generator?",options:[{text:"Less than 1 minute",correct:!1,feedback:"Far too slow."},{text:"Less than 10 seconds (Type 10)",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct."},{text:"Less than 1 hour",correct:!1,feedback:"Far too slow."},{text:"Less than half an hour",correct:!1,feedback:"Far too slow."}]},{question:'What does "Not dead until warm and dead" mean?',options:[{text:"It has no meaning",correct:!1,feedback:"It has an important meaning."},{text:"Before death is declared, a patient with severe hypothermia must be rewarmed and CPR continued (generally until about 32°C)",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct."},{text:"All hypothermia patients die",correct:!1,feedback:"Incorrect."},{text:"It applies only to mild hypothermia",correct:!1,feedback:"It specifically concerns severe hypothermia."}]}]}],t={lessons:e};export{t as default,e as lessons};
